// Original ZUNO reference excerpts. Fiction excluded from factual grounding.
export const library=[{"id":"solar-system","title":"Our incredible solar system","question":"What is in our solar system?","words":"planets orbit earth sun solar system space astronomy","levels":{"6-8":"Our Sun is a star. Eight planets travel around it. Earth is one of them! The path a planet follows around the Sun is called an orbit. Each planet has its own amazing story.","9-11":"Our solar system is the Sun and everything held by its gravity: eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids and comets. The four inner planets have rocky surfaces. The outer planets are much larger and very different from Earth.","12-14":"The solar system formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a cloud of gas and dust. Gravity gathered most of the material into the Sun, while the remaining disc formed planets and smaller bodies. The system includes terrestrial planets, gas giants, ice giants and dwarf planets."},"sections":[["One star, many worlds","The Sun supplies the light and energy that help make life on Earth possible. Planets do not make their own visible light like stars do; they reflect sunlight."],["A journey in order","Moving out from the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. Pluto is a dwarf planet."],["Try thinking like a scientist","Why might a planet farther from the Sun take longer to complete an orbit? Explore the model and compare the paths."]]},{"id":"mars","title":"Could we visit Mars?","question":"Why is Mars red?","words":"mars red planet mission rover space","levels":{"6-8":"Mars is a rocky planet with rusty red dust. Robots called rovers explore its surface. Mars is cold, and its air is very different from the air we breathe. People would need special suits and shelters to visit.","9-11":"Mars looks red because iron minerals in its soil have rusted. Rovers study its rocks for clues about ancient water. Its thin atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide, so a human visitor would need oxygen, warmth and protection.","12-14":"Mars is a cold terrestrial planet with a thin, carbon-dioxide-rich atmosphere. Evidence from channels, minerals and sedimentary rocks suggests liquid water flowed on its surface long ago. Missions investigate its past habitability; finding ancient water is not the same as finding life."},"sections":[["Robot explorers","Rovers carry cameras and scientific instruments. Teams on Earth plan their journeys, but a radio message takes time to travel between the planets."],["A very different home","Mars has lower gravity than Earth and a day that is slightly longer than ours. Its year lasts about 687 Earth days."],["Design challenge","Imagine a Mars home. How would you provide clean air, water, energy and protection from the cold? Sketch your ideas on paper."]]},{"id":"sun","title":"Meet our nearest star","question":"Is the Sun a star?","words":"sun star light energy solar","levels":{"6-8":"Yes! The Sun is our nearest star. It gives Earth light and warmth. It looks much bigger than the other stars because it is much closer. Never look straight at the Sun: it can hurt your eyes.","9-11":"The Sun is a star: a huge sphere of very hot plasma. Deep inside, hydrogen joins to form helium and releases energy. That energy eventually leaves as light and heat. Never look directly at the Sun, even during an eclipse without proper protection.","12-14":"The Sun is a main-sequence star powered by nuclear fusion in its core. Hydrogen nuclei combine into helium, converting a small amount of mass into energy. The energy travels outward before radiating into space. Direct solar viewing can permanently damage the eyes."},"sections":[["Energy for life","Plants capture some sunlight through photosynthesis. Many food chains begin with plants."],["Day and night","Earth rotates. Your part of Earth has daytime when it faces the Sun, and night when it faces away."],["Notice safely","With a grown-up, notice how a tree’s shadow changes during the day. Stay in the shade and never stare at the Sun."]]},{"id":"elephants","title":"The elephant’s secret toolkit","question":"How do elephants use their trunks?","words":"elephant trunk wildlife animals kenya savannah mammal","levels":{"6-8":"An elephant uses its trunk to smell, breathe, pick things up and drink. It sucks water into its trunk, then squirts it into its mouth. Big ears help it lose heat. Elephants live in families and learn from each other.","9-11":"An elephant’s trunk is an amazingly flexible nose and upper lip. It contains many muscles and no bones. Elephants use it for food, water, scent and greeting one another. Their large ears help release body heat.","12-14":"The elephant trunk is a muscular hydrostat: a flexible structure that moves without bones. It combines fine control with strength. African elephant species play important ecological roles, opening paths, dispersing seeds and changing vegetation as they feed."},"sections":[["Family matters","Elephants form strong social bonds. Older family members can help the group find water and remember routes."],["Nature’s gardeners","Seeds can travel inside an elephant and be left in its dung. This helps some plants spread to new places."],["Respect wildlife","Watch wild animals from a safe distance with a qualified guide. Never approach, feed or touch a wild elephant."]]},{"id":"rhinos","title":"Meet Kenya’s black rhino","question":"What is special about a black rhino?","words":"rhino black rhino kenya wildlife conservation animals","levels":{"6-8":"Black rhinos eat leaves and twigs. Their pointed upper lip helps them grab food from bushes. They have a strong sense of smell. Kenya protects rhinos in special areas where rangers work to keep them safe.","9-11":"Black rhinos are browsers: they feed mainly on leaves and twigs from shrubs. Their hooked upper lip helps them select food. Despite their names, black and white rhinos are both usually grey. Conservation work helps protect these threatened animals.","12-14":"Black rhinos are critically endangered browsers. Their prehensile upper lip is adapted to selecting leaves and shoots. Habitat protection, monitoring and anti-poaching work are important to recovery. Names such as “black” and “white” are not a reliable guide to their skin colour."},"sections":[["Built for browsing","Compare a pointed black-rhino lip with the broad mouth of a white rhino, which mainly grazes on grass."],["Every animal has a role","Browsing changes vegetation. Protecting a species also means caring for the habitat and the other living things it supports."],["Become a careful observer","What could an animal’s mouth tell you about its food? Compare drawings of a rhino, elephant and giraffe."]]},{"id":"rift-valley","title":"A giant story beneath Kenya","question":"How did the Great Rift Valley form?","words":"kenya great rift valley earth tectonic volcano geography","levels":{"6-8":"Earth’s hard outer layer is made of huge moving pieces. In parts of East Africa, the ground is slowly stretching. Over a very long time this has helped make valleys, mountains and volcanoes. Kenya is part of this amazing story.","9-11":"The East African Rift formed as tectonic forces stretched and thinned Earth’s crust. Blocks of land moved along faults, helping create deep valleys. Volcanoes and lakes are part of the landscape, but the changes happen over millions of years.","12-14":"The East African Rift is an active continental rift system. Extension thins the lithosphere and forms faults, subsiding basins and volcanic activity. The rift records complex tectonic processes operating over millions of years rather than a single sudden crack."},"sections":[["Slow changes, big landscapes","Tectonic plates move slowly, but their effects add up over geological time. Mountains, valleys and volcanoes tell parts of the story."],["Look closer to home","Kenya’s rift landscape includes lakes and volcanic landforms. Each place has its own geological and ecological history."],["Make a landscape","Use a sheet of paper to draw a valley, a lake and a volcano. Add labels explaining how each differs."]]},{"id":"water-cycle","title":"The never-ending water adventure","question":"Where does rain come from?","words":"water rain clouds evaporation condensation precipitation cycle weather","levels":{"6-8":"The Sun warms water in rivers, lakes and the ocean. Some water becomes an invisible gas called water vapour. It rises and cools. Tiny drops can form clouds. When drops grow heavy enough, water falls as rain.","9-11":"Water moves between the atmosphere, land and oceans. Evaporation turns liquid water into vapour. Cooling can cause condensation into cloud droplets. Rain and other precipitation return water to the surface, where it can flow, soak into soil or be used by plants.","12-14":"The water cycle transfers water among atmosphere, surface and subsurface stores. Solar energy drives evaporation and transpiration; condensation forms droplets or ice crystals. Precipitation, runoff, infiltration and groundwater flow redistribute water. There is no single starting point."},"sections":[["Clouds are not just vapour","Water vapour is invisible. The clouds you see contain tiny liquid drops, ice crystals, or both."],["Water takes many paths","A raindrop might soak into the soil, reach a stream, become groundwater or be absorbed by a plant."],["Observe without waste","Notice puddles after rain. Revisit the same spot later with a grown-up. What changed? You do not need to pour out extra water to test it."]]},{"id":"algorithms","title":"Think like a coder","question":"What is an algorithm?","words":"code coding algorithm robot programming sequence computer technology","levels":{"6-8":"An algorithm is a set of steps. Think about getting ready for school: put on socks, then put on shoes! Order matters. A computer follows instructions, so we need to make the steps clear.","9-11":"An algorithm is a clear set of steps for a task. Programmers turn algorithms into code. A sequence puts steps in order, a loop repeats them, and a condition chooses what happens next. Finding and fixing a mistake is called debugging.","12-14":"An algorithm describes a method for solving a problem. Programs implement algorithms using constructs such as sequence, iteration and selection. Testing different inputs helps reveal edge cases. Efficiency matters, but first the algorithm must produce the intended result."},"sections":[["Be precise","“Go over there” may be confusing. “Move forward two squares, then turn right” gives a robot clearer instructions."],["Mistakes help us learn","When instructions do not work, check one step at a time. A bug is a problem to investigate, not a reason to give up."],["Your first program","Open Code a Route in Play & Learn. Build a sequence, run it, then change it until the rover reaches the star."]]},{"id":"ai","title":"How does AI learn patterns?","question":"What is artificial intelligence?","words":"ai artificial intelligence computer learning machine technology pattern","levels":{"6-8":"AI is a kind of computer technology. It can find patterns and help people with some tasks. It is not a person and does not know everything. Ask a trusted grown-up when you are unsure, and keep personal information private.","9-11":"Artificial intelligence is technology that carries out tasks such as recognising patterns or generating text. Many AI systems learn patterns from examples. Their answers can contain mistakes. AI does not replace your own thinking, a teacher or a trusted grown-up.","12-14":"Many modern AI systems use machine learning to fit statistical patterns from training examples. Generative models create outputs using those learned patterns rather than verifying every claim. Their limitations include factual errors, bias and uncertainty. Checking sources is an essential skill."},"sections":[["A helper, not a human","AI can generate words that sound friendly, but it does not have human feelings or understanding. You are always free to stop using it."],["Keep private things private","Do not share your full name, school, address, phone number, passwords or photos in an AI conversation."],["Check a claim","Pick one surprising claim from a digital tool. Can you find the same fact in a reliable book or trusted reference?"]]},{"id":"coral","title":"A city made by tiny animals","question":"Is coral an animal?","words":"coral reef ocean sea animals marine kenya coast","levels":{"6-8":"A coral reef is full of tiny animals called coral polyps. Many corals build hard skeletons. Lots of sea creatures find food and shelter around reefs. Kenya’s coast has wonderful marine life to discover.","9-11":"Coral polyps are small animals. Many reef-building corals have tiny algae living in their tissues. The algae use sunlight to make food and share some with the coral. Over time, coral skeletons help form reefs that shelter many species.","12-14":"Reef-building corals are colonial animals that deposit calcium carbonate skeletons. Many have a symbiotic relationship with photosynthetic algae. Heat stress can disrupt this relationship, causing bleaching. Bleached coral is stressed and may recover, but prolonged stress can kill it."},"sections":[["A busy neighbourhood","Reefs provide shelter and feeding areas for fish and many other animals."],["A delicate balance","Warmer seas, pollution and physical damage can harm reefs. Protecting clean water helps marine habitats."],["Leave only bubbles","If you visit the coast with a grown-up, do not step on coral or collect living marine animals. Observe them where they live."]]},{"id":"wangari","title":"One woman. Millions of trees.","question":"Who was Wangari Maathai?","words":"wangari maathai kenya trees green belt movement history people environment","levels":{"6-8":"Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan scientist and changemaker. She helped people plant trees and care for the land. She showed how small actions, done together, can make a big difference.","9-11":"Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement in Kenya in 1977. It brought communities, especially women, together to plant trees and care for the environment. In 2004 she became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize.","12-14":"Professor Wangari Maathai connected environmental conservation, women’s empowerment, democracy and peace. The Green Belt Movement, founded in 1977, used community tree planting as a practical response to environmental damage and livelihoods challenges. She received the 2004 Nobel Peace Prize."},"sections":[["A living connection","Trees can provide habitat, protect soil and contribute to local livelihoods. The right tree belongs in the right place."],["Ideas become action","Community projects begin by listening to the people affected and working together on practical solutions."],["Your small action","With a grown-up, learn about a tree near your home. What animals use it? Draw it and label what you notice."]]},{"id":"patterns","title":"Find the pattern. Make your own.","question":"What is a repeating pattern?","words":"art pattern creativity shapes drawing sequence","levels":{"6-8":"A pattern is something that follows a rule. Circle, square, circle, square: what comes next? You can make a pattern using shapes, colours, sounds or movements.","9-11":"A repeating pattern has a unit that appears again and again. Patterns can also grow or change according to a rule. Artists and designers use patterns to create rhythm and structure.","12-14":"Patterns may be repeating, growing or recursive. Identifying the underlying rule helps us describe and predict a sequence. In visual design, symmetry, translation and rotation can build complex patterns from simple elements."},"sections":[["Look around","Notice patterns in a basket, a fabric, floor tiles or leaves. Describe what repeats without assuming all designs have the same meaning."],["Create a rule","Draw a circle, two squares, then another circle and two squares. Keep repeating your unit."],["Change one thing","Try a new colour or rotate a shape. How does the pattern feel different?"]]},{"id":"fractions","title":"A whole new way to share","question":"What is a fraction?","words":"math maths fraction half quarter equal parts sharing numbers","levels":{"6-8":"Imagine a chapati cut into two equal pieces. Each piece is one half. Cut the same chapati into four equal pieces, and each is one quarter. Fractions help us talk about equal parts of a whole.","9-11":"A fraction describes a number of equal parts. In 3/4, the denominator 4 tells us the whole has four equal parts, and the numerator 3 tells us we have three of them. Two quarters and one half describe the same amount.","12-14":"Fractions represent ratios of integers, with a nonzero denominator. Equivalent fractions describe the same value: 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. When adding fractions with different denominators, express them with a common denominator first."},"sections":[["Equal parts matter","Two pieces that have different sizes are not both one half of the same chapati. The sizes, not just the number of pieces, matter."],["Same amount, different names","Shade two of four equal squares. That is 2/4 of the shape, which is the same amount as 1/2."],["Try it on paper","Draw a rectangle. Divide it into eight equal boxes and shade four. What fraction have you shaded? Can you name it another way?"]]},{"id":"internet","title":"The internet is a team effort","question":"How does the internet work?","words":"the internet is a team effort how does the internet work? future lab · connections tech","levels":{"6-8":"The internet joins many computer networks. A message can travel through cables and wireless connections to reach another computer. It is not all stored inside your tablet!","9-11":"The internet connects networks. Devices send information in small pieces called packets. Routers help these pieces travel, and software puts the useful information together at the destination.","12-14":"The internet is a network of networks. Packets carry data and addressing information; routers forward them between networks. The web is one service using this infrastructure, alongside others such as email."},"sections":[["A website needs a home","A server responds to a browser’s request. Your home Wi-Fi is one link in a much larger system. A Wi-Fi connection alone does not guarantee access to the wider internet."],["Think about a missing link","If the classroom Wi-Fi works but a website does not, list two possible reasons. One observation is not enough to identify the cause."]]},{"id":"sensors","title":"How can a machine notice things?","question":"What is a sensor?","words":"how can a machine notice things? what is a sensor? future lab · inventing tech","levels":{"6-8":"A sensor helps a machine notice something, like light, movement or temperature. A program can use that information to decide what happens next.","9-11":"A sensor turns a physical change into a signal a machine can use. A light sensor can help a device respond to brightness. The program decides what to do with the reading.","12-14":"Sensors measure aspects of the physical world and produce signals that a system can process. Readings depend on calibration, placement, range and conditions; a number is not automatically an accurate description of the environment."},"sections":[["Input, rule, output","In a simple automatic lamp, brightness is an input, a threshold is part of the rule, and switching a light is an output."],["Test your idea","Would your lamp react to a shadow passing over its sensor? How could you avoid rapid switching? Think it through on paper."]]},{"id":"data","title":"Numbers that tell a useful story","question":"What is data?","words":"numbers that tell a useful story what is data? future lab · think clearly tech","levels":{"6-8":"Data is information we collect. You could count red, blue and green pencils. A clear question helps you know what to count.","9-11":"Data can include counts, measurements, words and categories. To use it well, ask where it came from, what was measured and what might be missing.","12-14":"Data represents observations or recorded information. Its usefulness depends on definitions, collection methods, missing values and context. A striking pattern is a reason to investigate, not automatic proof of a cause."},"sections":[["Choose a clear question","Counting books by colour answers a different question from counting them by topic. Decide what each category means before collecting information."],["Look for what is missing","A survey of your reading club describes that group. It may not represent children who are not in the club."]]},{"id":"online-safety","title":"Your pause-and-check toolkit","question":"How can I stay safer online?","words":"your pause-and-check toolkit how can i stay safer online? future lab · digital kindness tech","levels":{"6-8":"Keep your private details private. If a message worries you or asks you to keep an uncomfortable secret, tell a trusted grown-up. You do not have to reply.","9-11":"Pause before sharing a name, school, photo, location or password. Ask a trusted grown-up about confusing links or messages. If something upsetting happens, it is okay to stop and ask for help.","12-14":"Think about what information a service needs, what it can reveal and who can access it. Use privacy settings with a parent or guardian. Pressure, secrecy or urgency in a message is a reason to pause, not proof that you must act."},"sections":[["Check with someone you trust","A familiar-looking account or logo is not enough to prove a message is genuine. Do not test a suspicious link yourself."],["Kindness belongs online too","Ask permission before sharing someone else’s picture. Being curious does not mean being entitled to another person’s private details."]]},{"id":"electricity","title":"The energy behind the switch","question":"Where does electricity come from?","words":"the energy behind the switch where does electricity come from? how things work · energy science","levels":{"6-8":"Electricity helps lights, fans and computers work. People can produce electrical energy using sunlight, moving water, wind and other energy sources. Never open a plug or put anything into a socket.","9-11":"Electrical energy can be produced by converting other forms of energy. Many generators use motion, while solar cells can convert sunlight directly into electricity. Real electrical systems need trained people and safety protections.","12-14":"Electricity is an energy carrier. Generation converts other forms of energy into electrical energy; it does not create energy from nothing. Different generation methods have different materials, environmental effects and operating constraints."},"sections":[["Generation and storage differ","A battery stores energy chemically and can supply electrical energy. It is not the same thing as a power station or a solar panel."],["Try the virtual circuit","Change the resistance and voltage in the on-screen model. Its numbers describe an idealised resistor, not instructions for building a real circuit."]]},{"id":"solar-energy","title":"Can sunshine power a school?","question":"How does solar electricity work?","words":"can sunshine power a school? how does solar electricity work? our future · clean energy science","levels":{"6-8":"Solar cells can turn sunlight into electricity. A school with solar panels still needs the right equipment and grown-ups trained to install it.","9-11":"Solar photovoltaic cells convert sunlight directly into electricity. How much a system produces depends on conditions such as available sunlight and shading. Batteries, if included, can store some energy for later.","12-14":"Photovoltaic cells use semiconductor materials to convert light into electrical energy. System output depends on irradiance, temperature, orientation, shading and other losses. Installed capacity is not the same as the energy produced over a day."},"sections":[["A shadow changes the question","A sunny climate alone does not tell you how much energy a particular roof system will produce. Nearby trees and buildings can cast shadows."],["Think about the school day","List which devices need electricity and when. How might their needs differ from the times when sunlight is strongest?"]]},{"id":"wow-venus","title":"A year before a full spin?","question":"Can a planet’s full spin take longer than its year?","words":"a year before a full spin? can a planet’s full spin take longer than its year? little wow · space wows","levels":{"6-8":"Venus takes longer to turn all the way around once than to travel around the Sun once. What a slow spinner!","9-11":"Venus completes one rotation in about 243 Earth days, but one orbit takes about 225. A spin and a trip around the Sun are different motions.","12-14":"Venus’s sidereal rotation period is about 243 Earth days, longer than its roughly 225-day orbital period. Its sunrise-to-sunrise solar day is a different interval."},"sections":[["Try the idea","Imagine a toy that turns slowly while you carry it around a circle. Which movement decides its year?"]]},{"id":"wow-butterfly","title":"Tasting with your feet?","question":"Can butterflies taste with their feet?","words":"tasting with your feet? can butterflies taste with their feet? little wow · nature wows","levels":{"6-8":"Some butterflies use sensors on their feet to check plants. Your feet cannot do that!","9-11":"Butterflies have chemical sensors on their feet. A female can use them to help check whether a plant is suitable for laying eggs.","12-14":"Contact chemoreceptors on butterflies’ feet detect plant chemicals. This can help females select host plants for their caterpillars; it is not simply the same as a human tasting a snack."},"sections":[["Wonder a little more","Why might choosing the right plant matter for a caterpillar that will hatch there?"]]},{"id":"wow-coral","title":"That reef is made by animals.","question":"Is coral really an animal?","words":"that reef is made by animals. is coral really an animal? little wow · ocean wows","levels":{"6-8":"Corals are animals. Many tiny coral polyps help build the hard structures that become reefs.","9-11":"A coral may look like a colourful rock, but its living polyps are animals. Many reef-building corals make hard skeletons.","12-14":"Reef-building coral colonies contain animal polyps that deposit calcium carbonate skeletons. Their plant-like appearance does not make them plants."},"sections":[["Keep wondering","What clues would you look for before deciding whether something is living?"]]},{"id":"wow-sunlight","title":"Sunshine has travel time.","question":"Does sunlight reach Earth instantly?","words":"sunshine has travel time. does sunlight reach earth instantly? little wow · space wows","levels":{"6-8":"Sunlight takes about eight minutes to travel from the Sun to Earth. Even light needs time for a journey that big.","9-11":"Light travels very fast, but the Sun is far away. The trip from the Sun’s surface to Earth takes roughly eight minutes and twenty seconds.","12-14":"At an average Earth–Sun distance of about one astronomical unit, light’s travel time is roughly 499 seconds. Distance varies, so this is an approximate value."},"sections":[["A useful question","If light came from farther away, would you expect its journey to take more time or less?"]]},{"id":"wow-rainbow","title":"A rainbow can be a circle.","question":"Is a rainbow always half a circle?","words":"a rainbow can be a circle. is a rainbow always half a circle? little wow · light wows","levels":{"6-8":"A full rainbow is a circle. From the ground we usually see only part of it.","9-11":"Sunlight and water drops can make a circular rainbow. The ground often hides the lower part from our view.","12-14":"Rainbow geometry depends on the observer, the light and droplets. From suitable elevated viewpoints, the full circle can be visible; the familiar bow is only part of the pattern."},"sections":[["Notice your viewpoint","How could changing where you stand change which part of a pattern you can see?"]]},{"id":"wow-packets","title":"A message can travel in pieces.","question":"Does an internet message always travel as one piece?","words":"a message can travel in pieces. does an internet message always travel as one piece? little wow · tech wows","levels":{"6-8":"A digital message can be split into small pieces for its journey. Computers use rules to handle the pieces.","9-11":"Information on the internet travels in packets. Network protocols help devices send and make sense of the data.","12-14":"Packet-switched networks forward addressed chunks of data. Delivery order and reliability are handled differently by different protocols, so the “little envelopes” comparison has limits."},"sections":[["Ask a designer’s question","What could a system do if a needed piece did not arrive?"]]},{"id":"wow-trunk","title":"A nose that can pick things up.","question":"Is an elephant’s trunk just a long nose?","words":"a nose that can pick things up. is an elephant’s trunk just a long nose? little wow · wildlife wows","levels":{"6-8":"An elephant’s trunk is its nose and upper lip combined. It can smell and pick up things too.","9-11":"An elephant uses its trunk for breathing, smelling, handling food and other tasks. It is a flexible combination of nose and upper lip.","12-14":"The elephant trunk is a muscular hydrostat, combining fine manipulation and strength without a bony internal support. One structure serves many functions."},"sections":[["Observe, don’t approach","Watch wildlife only from a safe distance with a qualified guide. A clever adaptation is not an invitation to touch."]]},{"id":"wow-bugs","title":"A mistake can be a clue.","question":"What is debugging?","words":"a mistake can be a clue. what is debugging? little wow · tech wows","levels":{"6-8":"When code does the wrong thing, finding and fixing the problem is called debugging. A mistake can teach you something.","9-11":"Debugging means investigating why a program behaves differently from what you intended. Clear predictions and small changes help.","12-14":"Debugging compares expected and observed behaviour to identify causes. A successful rerun is useful evidence, but one test does not prove a program works in every situation."},"sections":[["Try the habit","Before changing code, write down what you expect each step to do."]]},{"id":"fact-our-sun-is-a-star","title":"Our Sun is a star","question":"Tell me about Our Sun is a star","words":"Our Sun is a star space","levels":{"6-8":"The Sun is a star, just like the tiny lights you see at night.","9-11":"The Sun looks much bigger than other stars because it is much closer to Earth.","12-14":"Our Sun is an ordinary star whose apparent size comes from its proximity, rather than exceptional physical size."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mercury-has-a-quick-year","title":"Mercury has a quick year","question":"Tell me about Mercury has a quick year","words":"Mercury has a quick year space","levels":{"6-8":"Mercury goes around the Sun faster than Earth does.","9-11":"A year on Mercury lasts about 88 Earth days because its orbit is close to the Sun.","12-14":"Mercury completes an orbit in roughly 88 Earth days, giving it the shortest orbital period among the eight planets."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-venus-is-hotter-than-mercury","title":"Venus is hotter than Mercury","question":"Tell me about Venus is hotter than Mercury","words":"Venus is hotter than Mercury space","levels":{"6-8":"Venus is farther from the Sun than Mercury, yet it is hotter.","9-11":"Venus has a thick atmosphere that traps heat, making its surface hotter than Mercury’s.","12-14":"A strong greenhouse effect in Venus’s dense atmosphere makes it the hottest planetary surface in our solar system."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-earth-turns-toward-daylight","title":"Earth turns toward daylight","question":"Tell me about Earth turns toward daylight","words":"Earth turns toward daylight space","levels":{"6-8":"It is daytime when your part of Earth faces the Sun.","9-11":"Earth’s rotation brings different places into sunlight and then into darkness, creating day and night.","12-14":"Day and night result from Earth’s axial rotation; the Sun does not travel around Earth once each day."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mars-wears-rusty-dust","title":"Mars wears rusty dust","question":"Tell me about Mars wears rusty dust","words":"Mars wears rusty dust space","levels":{"6-8":"Rusty minerals help make Mars look red.","9-11":"Iron-bearing minerals in Martian rocks and dust have oxidised, giving much of the planet its reddish colour.","12-14":"Mars’s characteristic colour comes mainly from oxidised iron minerals, although its landscapes contain many other colours too."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-jupiter-is-the-giant","title":"Jupiter is the giant","question":"Tell me about Jupiter is the giant","words":"Jupiter is the giant space","levels":{"6-8":"Jupiter is the biggest planet in our solar system.","9-11":"Jupiter is much wider and more massive than Earth, but it has no solid surface like ours.","12-14":"Jupiter is a gas giant dominated by hydrogen and helium, rather than a rocky planet with an accessible solid surface."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-saturn-has-icy-rings","title":"Saturn has icy rings","question":"Tell me about Saturn has icy rings","words":"Saturn has icy rings space","levels":{"6-8":"Saturn’s beautiful rings are made of many separate pieces.","9-11":"The rings around Saturn contain huge numbers of ice particles, with some rocky material and dust.","12-14":"Saturn’s rings are orbiting particles, mostly water ice, rather than one continuous solid disc."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-uranus-rolls-through-its-orbit","title":"Uranus rolls through its orbit","question":"Tell me about Uranus rolls through its orbit","words":"Uranus rolls through its orbit space","levels":{"6-8":"Uranus is tipped so far over that it looks as if it rolls around the Sun.","9-11":"Uranus’s rotation axis is tilted by about a right angle compared with most planets.","12-14":"Uranus has an extreme axial tilt, producing unusual seasonal patterns as its poles alternately face the Sun."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-neptune-is-a-windy-world","title":"Neptune is a windy world","question":"Tell me about Neptune is a windy world","words":"Neptune is a windy world space","levels":{"6-8":"Neptune is a very distant planet with powerful winds.","9-11":"Despite being far from the Sun, Neptune has an active atmosphere with fast-moving winds and storms.","12-14":"Neptune’s atmosphere remains dynamic even with weak sunlight; internal heat also contributes to its energy system."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-moonlight-is-borrowed-sunlight","title":"Moonlight is borrowed sunlight","question":"Tell me about Moonlight is borrowed sunlight","words":"Moonlight is borrowed sunlight space","levels":{"6-8":"The Moon shines by reflecting light from the Sun.","9-11":"The Moon does not make its own visible light; sunlight reflected from its surface reaches our eyes.","12-14":"Moonlight is reflected solar radiation. Changing viewing geometry produces phases without the Moon changing its own shape."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-moon-shows-changing-phases","title":"The Moon shows changing phases","question":"Tell me about The Moon shows changing phases","words":"The Moon shows changing phases space","levels":{"6-8":"The Moon can look like a thin smile or a full circle.","9-11":"We see different amounts of the Moon’s sunlit half as it travels around Earth.","12-14":"Ordinary lunar phases depend on the relative positions of Sun, Earth and Moon, rather than Earth’s shadow."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-asteroids-are-rocky-leftovers","title":"Asteroids are rocky leftovers","question":"Tell me about Asteroids are rocky leftovers","words":"Asteroids are rocky leftovers space","levels":{"6-8":"Many asteroids are rocky pieces that travel around the Sun.","9-11":"Most asteroids orbit between Mars and Jupiter, although some follow other paths.","12-14":"Asteroids preserve material from early solar-system formation; the main belt is a population of separate objects, not a solid ring."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-comets-carry-ice","title":"Comets carry ice","question":"Tell me about Comets carry ice","words":"Comets carry ice space","levels":{"6-8":"A comet contains ice as well as dust and rocky material.","9-11":"When a comet approaches the Sun, warming releases gas and dust that can form a glowing coma and tails.","12-14":"Solar heating causes volatile material to escape a comet’s nucleus, while solar radiation and wind shape its tails."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-comet-tail-points-away","title":"A comet tail points away","question":"Tell me about A comet tail points away","words":"A comet tail points away space","levels":{"6-8":"A comet’s tail usually stretches away from the Sun.","9-11":"Comet tails are pushed outward by sunlight and the solar wind, so they do not simply trail behind the comet.","12-14":"The ion tail follows the solar-wind direction closely; the dust tail can curve because particles follow different orbits."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-shooting-star-is-not-a-star","title":"A shooting star is not a star","question":"Tell me about A shooting star is not a star","words":"A shooting star is not a star space","levels":{"6-8":"A shooting star is a small space object glowing in Earth’s air.","9-11":"The bright streak called a meteor forms when material from space enters the atmosphere at high speed.","12-14":"Meteor light comes from energetic atmospheric entry; a fragment that reaches the ground is called a meteorite."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-solar-system-has-eight-planets","title":"The solar system has eight planets","question":"Tell me about The solar system has eight planets","words":"The solar system has eight planets space","levels":{"6-8":"Earth is one of eight planets that go around our Sun.","9-11":"The eight planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.","12-14":"The current classification recognises eight planets; dwarf planets such as Pluto form a separate category, not an extra ninth planet."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-stars-have-different-colours","title":"Stars have different colours","question":"Tell me about Stars have different colours","words":"Stars have different colours space","levels":{"6-8":"Some stars look reddish, while others look bluish.","9-11":"A star’s colour gives scientists a clue about its surface temperature: blue stars are generally hotter than red ones.","12-14":"Stellar colour relates to surface temperature, though dust, instruments and a star’s chemical composition can affect observed light."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-space-is-not-completely-empty","title":"Space is not completely empty","question":"Tell me about Space is not completely empty","words":"Space is not completely empty space","levels":{"6-8":"Space has scattered gas, dust and tiny particles.","9-11":"Even between planets and stars, space contains thinly spread matter along with light and other radiation.","12-14":"A vacuum is not necessarily absolute emptiness; interplanetary and interstellar regions contain extremely low-density material."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-an-eclipse-needs-alignment","title":"An eclipse needs alignment","question":"Tell me about An eclipse needs alignment","words":"An eclipse needs alignment space","levels":{"6-8":"An eclipse happens when objects in space line up and block light.","9-11":"A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between Earth and the Sun, hiding part or all of the Sun from some places.","12-14":"Eclipses require particular orbital alignments. The Moon’s tilted orbit is why a solar eclipse does not happen every new moon."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-telescopes-collect-light","title":"Telescopes collect light","question":"Tell me about Telescopes collect light","words":"Telescopes collect light space","levels":{"6-8":"A telescope gathers light so we can study faraway things.","9-11":"Larger telescope mirrors or lenses can collect more light, revealing faint objects that eyes alone cannot see.","12-14":"A telescope’s collecting area and resolving power affect what it can reveal; magnification alone does not determine its scientific usefulness."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-clouds-hold-tiny-droplets","title":"Clouds hold tiny droplets","question":"Tell me about Clouds hold tiny droplets","words":"Clouds hold tiny droplets science","levels":{"6-8":"Clouds are made of tiny water drops or ice crystals.","9-11":"Water vapour is invisible; a visible cloud contains suspended liquid droplets, ice crystals or both.","12-14":"Clouds become visible when condensed droplets or ice crystals scatter light, rather than because gaseous water itself is white."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-water-can-leave-a-puddle","title":"Water can leave a puddle","question":"Tell me about Water can leave a puddle","words":"Water can leave a puddle science","levels":{"6-8":"A puddle can slowly shrink even when nobody drinks it.","9-11":"Liquid water can evaporate into invisible water vapour, including on days when it is not boiling.","12-14":"Evaporation occurs at a liquid surface across a range of temperatures; boiling is a different process involving bubbles within the liquid."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rain-starts-in-clouds","title":"Rain starts in clouds","question":"Tell me about Rain starts in clouds","words":"Rain starts in clouds science","levels":{"6-8":"Rain falls when water drops in clouds grow large enough.","9-11":"Cloud droplets can join together until the resulting drops fall through the air as precipitation.","12-14":"Rain formation involves droplet growth or ice processes; cloud particles do not all fall as soon as they form."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-snowflakes-grow-from-ice","title":"Snowflakes grow from ice","question":"Tell me about Snowflakes grow from ice","words":"Snowflakes grow from ice science","levels":{"6-8":"Snowflakes are ice crystals that grow in cold clouds.","9-11":"Water vapour can become ice crystals, and their growth produces the branching shapes seen in many snowflakes.","12-14":"Temperature and moisture conditions influence ice-crystal growth, so not every snow crystal has the same intricate branching form."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-wind-is-moving-air","title":"Wind is moving air","question":"Tell me about Wind is moving air","words":"Wind is moving air science","levels":{"6-8":"Wind is air moving from one place to another.","9-11":"Differences in air pressure help drive winds, while Earth’s rotation changes their direction on large scales.","12-14":"Pressure gradients accelerate air; rotation, friction and local terrain influence the wind we eventually measure."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-thermometer-measures-temperature","title":"A thermometer measures temperature","question":"Tell me about A thermometer measures temperature","words":"A thermometer measures temperature science","levels":{"6-8":"A thermometer helps us find out how hot or cold something is.","9-11":"Thermometers measure temperature using a property that changes predictably, such as a liquid’s volume or an electrical signal.","12-14":"Temperature measurements require a calibrated response and suitable exposure; direct sunlight can distort an air-temperature reading."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-rain-gauge-catches-rainfall","title":"A rain gauge catches rainfall","question":"Tell me about A rain gauge catches rainfall","words":"A rain gauge catches rainfall science","levels":{"6-8":"A rain gauge collects rain so its amount can be measured.","9-11":"The depth collected in a suitable rain gauge represents rainfall at that location over a chosen time.","12-14":"Gauge design, siting and wind affect precipitation measurements, so one gauge does not perfectly represent an entire region."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-air-has-weight","title":"Air has weight","question":"Tell me about Air has weight","words":"Air has weight science","levels":{"6-8":"Air may be invisible, but it still presses on things.","9-11":"The weight of the atmosphere creates air pressure, which can be measured with a barometer.","12-14":"Atmospheric pressure depends on the overlying air column and varies with altitude and weather conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fog-is-a-low-cloud","title":"Fog is a low cloud","question":"Tell me about Fog is a low cloud","words":"Fog is a low cloud science","levels":{"6-8":"Fog is a cloud close to the ground.","9-11":"Like other clouds, fog contains tiny suspended droplets; it can make nearby objects difficult to see.","12-14":"Fog occurs when near-surface air reaches conditions that allow droplets to form, reducing visibility through light scattering."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-dew-forms-on-cool-surfaces","title":"Dew forms on cool surfaces","question":"Tell me about Dew forms on cool surfaces","words":"Dew forms on cool surfaces science","levels":{"6-8":"Tiny drops can appear on cool grass overnight.","9-11":"Dew forms when water vapour in the air condenses on a surface that has cooled enough.","12-14":"A surface below the local dew-point temperature can collect condensation even when no rain has fallen."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-frost-is-frozen-looking-crystal-growth","title":"Frost is frozen-looking crystal growth","question":"Tell me about Frost is frozen-looking crystal growth","words":"Frost is frozen-looking crystal growth science","levels":{"6-8":"Frost can cover a cold leaf with tiny ice crystals.","9-11":"Under suitable cold conditions, water vapour can deposit directly as ice on a surface.","12-14":"Hoarfrost grows through deposition of water vapour; frozen dew is another icy surface deposit with a different formation route."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-lightning-heats-the-air","title":"Lightning heats the air","question":"Tell me about Lightning heats the air","words":"Lightning heats the air science","levels":{"6-8":"Lightning is a powerful electrical discharge in the sky.","9-11":"Thunder happens because lightning rapidly heats surrounding air, causing it to expand and create a sound wave.","12-14":"Lightning’s rapid energy release produces an expanding pressure wave; thunder is the acoustic result, not clouds colliding."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-light-reaches-you-before-thunder","title":"Light reaches you before thunder","question":"Tell me about Light reaches you before thunder","words":"Light reaches you before thunder science","levels":{"6-8":"You often see lightning before you hear its thunder.","9-11":"Light travels far faster than sound in air, so the flash reaches your eyes before the thunder reaches your ears.","12-14":"The delay between a flash and thunder reflects different propagation speeds, not a delay between two separate storm events."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-rainbow-needs-light-and-water","title":"A rainbow needs light and water","question":"Tell me about A rainbow needs light and water","words":"A rainbow needs light and water science","levels":{"6-8":"A rainbow can appear when sunlight meets drops of water.","9-11":"Raindrops bend and internally reflect sunlight, separating colours toward an observer in the right position.","12-14":"Refraction, dispersion and internal reflection produce a rainbow; its apparent position depends on the observer and light source."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-rainbow-can-be-a-circle","title":"A rainbow can be a circle","question":"Tell me about A rainbow can be a circle","words":"A rainbow can be a circle science","levels":{"6-8":"From high enough above the ground, a rainbow can look circular.","9-11":"The familiar arch is only part of a circle; the ground usually blocks our view of the lower portion.","12-14":"A rainbow is centred opposite the light source relative to the observer, and elevated viewpoints can reveal its full circular geometry."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-weather-and-climate-differ","title":"Weather and climate differ","question":"Tell me about Weather and climate differ","words":"Weather and climate differ science","levels":{"6-8":"Weather is what the air is doing now; climate describes longer patterns.","9-11":"One rainy afternoon describes weather, while climate involves typical conditions and variation over many years.","12-14":"Climate is a statistical description of weather over extended periods, so a single unusual day cannot establish a climate trend."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-dark-cloud-bases-have-a-reason","title":"Dark cloud bases have a reason","question":"Tell me about Dark cloud bases have a reason","words":"Dark cloud bases have a reason science","levels":{"6-8":"A thick cloud can look dark underneath.","9-11":"A thick cloud scatters and absorbs incoming light, so less sunlight reaches its underside.","12-14":"A cloud’s apparent brightness depends on optical thickness, illumination and viewing direction, rather than on the colour of its water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mountains-can-change-rainfall","title":"Mountains can change rainfall","question":"Tell me about Mountains can change rainfall","words":"Mountains can change rainfall science","levels":{"6-8":"Mountains can help make one side wetter than the other.","9-11":"Air rising over mountains can cool and form clouds, leaving some downwind areas drier.","12-14":"Orographic lifting can enhance precipitation on windward slopes and contribute to rain shadows, though local conditions still matter."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sea-breezes-connect-land-and-water","title":"Sea breezes connect land and water","question":"Tell me about Sea breezes connect land and water","words":"Sea breezes connect land and water science","levels":{"6-8":"Coastal wind can blow in from the sea during the day.","9-11":"Land often warms faster than nearby water, helping create a daytime breeze from sea toward land.","12-14":"Differential heating changes local pressure patterns; sea-breeze circulation varies with background winds, coastline and terrain."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-weather-forecasts-use-evidence","title":"Weather forecasts use evidence","question":"Tell me about Weather forecasts use evidence","words":"Weather forecasts use evidence science","levels":{"6-8":"A forecast is a prediction built from weather observations.","9-11":"Weather stations, satellites and computer models help scientists predict what the atmosphere may do next.","12-14":"Forecasts combine observations with physical models and carry uncertainty, which usually grows for more distant prediction times."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-earth-has-layers","title":"Earth has layers","question":"Tell me about Earth has layers","words":"Earth has layers science","levels":{"6-8":"Earth has a thin outer crust and deeper layers underneath.","9-11":"Below Earth’s crust are the mantle and core, which differ in material properties and temperature.","12-14":"Earth’s interior is described using compositional and mechanical layers; the mantle is mostly solid rock that can deform over long times."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-crust-is-broken-into-plates","title":"The crust is broken into plates","question":"Tell me about The crust is broken into plates","words":"The crust is broken into plates science","levels":{"6-8":"Earth’s outer rocky shell is divided into moving plates.","9-11":"Tectonic plates move slowly and interact along boundaries, helping create mountains, volcanoes and earthquakes.","12-14":"Lithospheric plate motion reflects Earth’s internal dynamics; most noticeable deformation occurs near boundaries, though activity can also occur within plates."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mountains-can-rise-at-collisions","title":"Mountains can rise at collisions","question":"Tell me about Mountains can rise at collisions","words":"Mountains can rise at collisions science","levels":{"6-8":"Some mountains grow where large pieces of Earth’s crust push together.","9-11":"When tectonic plates converge, rocks can be folded, faulted and thickened into mountain ranges.","12-14":"Mountain building involves crustal shortening and uplift, followed by erosion; not every mountain forms through the same tectonic process."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-volcanoes-bring-material-upward","title":"Volcanoes bring material upward","question":"Tell me about Volcanoes bring material upward","words":"Volcanoes bring material upward science","levels":{"6-8":"A volcano is a place where hot material can reach Earth’s surface.","9-11":"Molten rock beneath the ground is magma; once it reaches the surface, it is called lava.","12-14":"Volcanic eruptions transfer magma, gases and fragmented material upward, with behaviour depending on composition and conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-earthquakes-release-stored-energy","title":"Earthquakes release stored energy","question":"Tell me about Earthquakes release stored energy","words":"Earthquakes release stored energy science","levels":{"6-8":"An earthquake makes the ground shake when rocks suddenly move.","9-11":"Stress can build along a fault until rocks slip, releasing energy as seismic waves.","12-14":"Fault rupture releases elastic strain energy; earthquake magnitude measures the event, while local shaking depends on distance and ground conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rocks-change-through-a-cycle","title":"Rocks change through a cycle","question":"Tell me about Rocks change through a cycle","words":"Rocks change through a cycle science","levels":{"6-8":"A rock can change into a different kind of rock over a long time.","9-11":"Melting, cooling, pressure, heat, weathering and burial connect the major rock groups in the rock cycle.","12-14":"The rock cycle has many possible pathways rather than a single fixed sequence followed by every rock."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-igneous-rocks-cool-from-melt","title":"Igneous rocks cool from melt","question":"Tell me about Igneous rocks cool from melt","words":"Igneous rocks cool from melt science","levels":{"6-8":"Some rocks begin as melted material that cools.","9-11":"Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and solidifies, often producing mineral crystals.","12-14":"Cooling rate and composition influence igneous texture: slow cooling often allows larger crystals to grow."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sedimentary-rocks-build-in-layers","title":"Sedimentary rocks build in layers","question":"Tell me about Sedimentary rocks build in layers","words":"Sedimentary rocks build in layers science","levels":{"6-8":"Sand and mud can become rock after they pile up.","9-11":"Many sedimentary rocks form when deposited particles are compacted and cemented over time.","12-14":"Sedimentary rocks can preserve depositional environments; some form chemically or biologically rather than from transported fragments alone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-metamorphic-rocks-are-changed-rocks","title":"Metamorphic rocks are changed rocks","question":"Tell me about Metamorphic rocks are changed rocks","words":"Metamorphic rocks are changed rocks science","levels":{"6-8":"Heat and pressure can change a rock without melting it completely.","9-11":"Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are altered by heat, pressure or reactive fluids.","12-14":"Metamorphism reorganises minerals and textures largely in the solid state, distinguishing it from melting and igneous crystallisation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-soil-is-more-than-dirt","title":"Soil is more than dirt","question":"Tell me about Soil is more than dirt","words":"Soil is more than dirt science","levels":{"6-8":"Soil contains tiny rock pieces, water, air and living things.","9-11":"Soil also contains decayed organic material, and its mixture helps support plants and many organisms.","12-14":"Soils develop through physical, chemical and biological processes; their properties vary with parent material, climate, organisms, relief and time."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-water-wears-rocks-away","title":"Water wears rocks away","question":"Tell me about Water wears rocks away","words":"Water wears rocks away science","levels":{"6-8":"Running water can slowly reshape rock and land.","9-11":"Streams move sediment and erode channels, gradually shaping valleys and other landforms.","12-14":"Erosion involves removal and transport, while weathering breaks material down in place; the two processes often work together."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-wind-can-carry-sand","title":"Wind can carry sand","question":"Tell me about Wind can carry sand","words":"Wind can carry sand science","levels":{"6-8":"Wind can pick up small particles and move them elsewhere.","9-11":"Wind-blown sediment can collect into dunes whose shapes change as particles move.","12-14":"Aeolian transport depends on particle size, surface conditions and wind strength; dunes migrate through erosion and deposition."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ice-can-carve-valleys","title":"Ice can carve valleys","question":"Tell me about Ice can carve valleys","words":"Ice can carve valleys science","levels":{"6-8":"A moving glacier can scrape and shape the ground.","9-11":"Glaciers carry rock fragments that help erode the land beneath them as ice flows downhill.","12-14":"Glacial abrasion and plucking reshape landscapes, while deposited sediment records parts of a glacier’s movement and retreat."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rivers-have-drainage-areas","title":"Rivers have drainage areas","question":"Tell me about Rivers have drainage areas","words":"Rivers have drainage areas science","levels":{"6-8":"Rain falling across a whole area can feed one river.","9-11":"A watershed is the land area whose water drains toward a common stream, lake or outlet.","12-14":"Watershed boundaries follow topographic divides, so activities far upstream can influence water and sediment downstream."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-groundwater-fills-small-spaces","title":"Groundwater fills small spaces","question":"Tell me about Groundwater fills small spaces","words":"Groundwater fills small spaces science","levels":{"6-8":"Water can be stored in spaces between underground grains and cracks.","9-11":"Groundwater occupies pores and fractures in suitable rocks and sediments, rather than usually forming underground rivers.","12-14":"Aquifers store and transmit groundwater; permeability controls movement, while porosity describes the proportion of open space."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-some-caves-grow-in-limestone","title":"Some caves grow in limestone","question":"Tell me about Some caves grow in limestone","words":"Some caves grow in limestone science","levels":{"6-8":"Water can slowly dissolve limestone and make caves.","9-11":"Slightly acidic water reacts with limestone, widening cracks and sometimes creating large cave systems.","12-14":"Carbonate dissolution forms many karst landscapes, while other cave types develop through different geological processes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sand-grains-tell-stories","title":"Sand grains tell stories","question":"Tell me about Sand grains tell stories","words":"Sand grains tell stories science","levels":{"6-8":"Sand can contain tiny pieces of rock, shell or coral.","9-11":"A beach’s sand reflects nearby materials, transport and the processes that produced its grains.","12-14":"Grain composition, rounding and size can provide clues about sediment origin and transport, without uniquely determining the whole history."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fossils-are-traces-of-past-life","title":"Fossils are traces of past life","question":"Tell me about Fossils are traces of past life","words":"Fossils are traces of past life science","levels":{"6-8":"A fossil is evidence of something that lived long ago.","9-11":"Fossils can include preserved body parts, footprints, burrows and other traces left by organisms.","12-14":"Fossil preservation is selective: the geological record is incomplete and often favours durable structures and suitable burial conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-continents-have-moved","title":"Continents have moved","question":"Tell me about Continents have moved","words":"Continents have moved science","levels":{"6-8":"The continents have not always been in the places we see today.","9-11":"Evidence from rocks, fossils and the seafloor shows that continents have changed position through plate motion.","12-14":"Matching geological evidence and modern measurements support continental motion; continents are carried within larger tectonic plates."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-map-is-a-model","title":"A map is a model","question":"Tell me about A map is a model","words":"A map is a model science","levels":{"6-8":"A map shows selected information about a place.","9-11":"Map symbols, scale and projection help represent a large curved world on a smaller surface.","12-14":"Every map makes choices about detail and distortion; a representation useful for one question may be unsuitable for another."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ocean-water-is-salty","title":"Ocean water is salty","question":"Tell me about Ocean water is salty","words":"Ocean water is salty ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Sea water has dissolved salts mixed into it.","9-11":"Weathering on land and processes beneath the sea supply dissolved substances that contribute to ocean salinity.","12-14":"Ocean salinity reflects inputs, removal processes and water movement; evaporation removes water while leaving most dissolved salts behind."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-ocean-covers-most-of-earth","title":"The ocean covers most of Earth","question":"Tell me about The ocean covers most of Earth","words":"The ocean covers most of Earth ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Most of Earth’s surface is covered by ocean.","9-11":"The connected global ocean covers about seven tenths of Earth’s surface.","12-14":"Named oceans are parts of one connected body of salt water, although circulation and geography create distinct regions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-waves-carry-energy","title":"Waves carry energy","question":"Tell me about Waves carry energy","words":"Waves carry energy ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A wave can move across water without carrying the same water all the way.","9-11":"Many surface waves are made by wind transferring energy to water.","12-14":"In many surface waves, water particles move in orbital paths while wave energy travels; currents involve a net flow of water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-tides-follow-gravity","title":"Tides follow gravity","question":"Tell me about Tides follow gravity","words":"Tides follow gravity ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Sea level rises and falls in a repeating pattern called the tides.","9-11":"The gravity of the Moon and Sun, together with Earth’s motion, helps produce ocean tides.","12-14":"Tidal patterns depend on gravitational forcing and basin response, so local tides are not identical at every coastline."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ocean-currents-move-water","title":"Ocean currents move water","question":"Tell me about Ocean currents move water","words":"Ocean currents move water ocean","levels":{"6-8":"The ocean has broad flows of water called currents.","9-11":"Winds, Earth’s rotation and differences in water density help drive ocean circulation.","12-14":"Surface and deep circulation redistribute heat, salt, nutrients and other substances, linking distant regions of the ocean."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-corals-are-animals","title":"Corals are animals","question":"Tell me about Corals are animals","words":"Corals are animals ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A coral is an animal, even if it looks like a plant or rock.","9-11":"Many reef-building corals are colonies of small animals called polyps that make hard skeletons.","12-14":"Reef-building coral polyps secrete calcium carbonate; accumulated skeletons help construct complex reef habitats over time."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-coral-has-tiny-partners","title":"Coral has tiny partners","question":"Tell me about Coral has tiny partners","words":"Coral has tiny partners ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Many corals share their home with tiny algae.","9-11":"Algae living in coral tissues use sunlight to make food and share some products with their coral hosts.","12-14":"The coral–algal partnership supports many shallow reefs, but not every coral species depends on photosynthetic symbionts."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kelp-is-a-large-alga","title":"Kelp is a large alga","question":"Tell me about Kelp is a large alga","words":"Kelp is a large alga ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Kelp looks like an underwater plant, but it is a kind of alga.","9-11":"Kelp forests provide food and shelter for many sea animals in suitable cool coastal waters.","12-14":"Large brown algae create structurally complex kelp forests; their distribution depends on light, nutrients, temperature and substrate."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-seagrass-is-a-flowering-plant","title":"Seagrass is a flowering plant","question":"Tell me about Seagrass is a flowering plant","words":"Seagrass is a flowering plant ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Some flowering plants grow completely under the sea.","9-11":"Seagrasses have roots, leaves and flowers, unlike the algae often called seaweed.","12-14":"Seagrass meadows are marine angiosperm habitats that can stabilise sediment, shelter animals and store carbon in their sediments."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-plankton-drift-with-water","title":"Plankton drift with water","question":"Tell me about Plankton drift with water","words":"Plankton drift with water ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Plankton are organisms that drift with water currents.","9-11":"Some plankton make food using sunlight, while others feed on other organisms.","12-14":"Plankton are defined mainly by limited ability to swim against currents, not by membership of one biological group."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-tiny-ocean-life-makes-oxygen","title":"Tiny ocean life makes oxygen","question":"Tell me about Tiny ocean life makes oxygen","words":"Tiny ocean life makes oxygen ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Tiny ocean organisms can make oxygen using sunlight.","9-11":"Photosynthetic plankton contribute a large share of the oxygen produced on Earth.","12-14":"Marine photosynthesis produces oxygen, but ocean organisms also consume it, so gross production is different from net accumulation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-whales-must-breathe-air","title":"Whales must breathe air","question":"Tell me about Whales must breathe air","words":"Whales must breathe air ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A whale lives in water but comes to the surface to breathe.","9-11":"Whales are mammals with lungs, and they breathe through blowholes on top of their heads.","12-14":"Whales evolved from land mammals; their diving adaptations do not replace the need to exchange gases with atmospheric air."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-dolphins-use-echoes","title":"Dolphins use echoes","question":"Tell me about Dolphins use echoes","words":"Dolphins use echoes ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A dolphin can use returning sounds to learn about things nearby.","9-11":"Many dolphins produce clicks and listen to echoes to detect objects and prey.","12-14":"Echolocation uses sound timing and other acoustic features; it is a sensory system rather than ordinary vision through water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sharks-have-cartilage-skeletons","title":"Sharks have cartilage skeletons","question":"Tell me about Sharks have cartilage skeletons","words":"Sharks have cartilage skeletons ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A shark’s skeleton is made mostly of cartilage rather than bone.","9-11":"Cartilage is a flexible supporting tissue, also found in parts of the human nose and ears.","12-14":"Sharks belong to the cartilaginous fishes; their skeletal tissues can be strengthened by mineral deposits without becoming typical bone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-octopuses-have-flexible-arms","title":"Octopuses have flexible arms","question":"Tell me about Octopuses have flexible arms","words":"Octopuses have flexible arms ocean","levels":{"6-8":"An octopus can bend its arms in many directions.","9-11":"Octopus arms contain muscles and no bones, allowing flexible movement and handling of objects.","12-14":"Muscular-hydrostat mechanics let octopus arms change shape while maintaining volume; distributed nervous control helps coordinate movement."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sea-stars-are-not-fish","title":"Sea stars are not fish","question":"Tell me about Sea stars are not fish","words":"Sea stars are not fish ocean","levels":{"6-8":"A sea star is an ocean animal, but it is not a fish.","9-11":"Sea stars belong to a group called echinoderms, along with sea urchins and sea cucumbers.","12-14":"Adult sea stars usually show radial body organisation and use a water vascular system, distinguishing them from vertebrate fishes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mangroves-live-at-the-edge","title":"Mangroves live at the edge","question":"Tell me about Mangroves live at the edge","words":"Mangroves live at the edge ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Mangrove trees grow where land meets salty coastal water.","9-11":"Mangroves have adaptations that help them survive salt and waterlogged soils.","12-14":"Mangrove species manage salinity and low-oxygen sediments in different ways; their roots also create important coastal habitat."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-estuaries-mix-fresh-and-salt-water","title":"Estuaries mix fresh and salt water","question":"Tell me about Estuaries mix fresh and salt water","words":"Estuaries mix fresh and salt water ocean","levels":{"6-8":"An estuary is a place where river water meets the sea.","9-11":"Fresh water and sea water mix in estuaries, creating changing conditions for plants and animals.","12-14":"Estuarine salinity varies with tides, river flow and mixing, so organisms often encounter strong environmental gradients."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-deep-water-gets-little-sunlight","title":"Deep water gets little sunlight","question":"Tell me about Deep water gets little sunlight","words":"Deep water gets little sunlight ocean","levels":{"6-8":"The deep ocean is dark because sunlight cannot reach very far.","9-11":"Light weakens with depth, leaving much of the deep sea without enough sunlight for photosynthesis.","12-14":"Light attenuation separates sunlit surface habitats from deeper systems that rely on sinking organic material or other energy sources."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-some-sea-life-makes-light","title":"Some sea life makes light","question":"Tell me about Some sea life makes light","words":"Some sea life makes light ocean","levels":{"6-8":"Some ocean animals can produce their own light.","9-11":"Bioluminescence is light made by a chemical reaction inside an organism or its microbial partners.","12-14":"Bioluminescent signals can serve different functions, including communication, camouflage and prey attraction, depending on the species."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-an-elephant-trunk-has-no-bones","title":"An elephant trunk has no bones","question":"Tell me about An elephant trunk has no bones","words":"An elephant trunk has no bones animals","levels":{"6-8":"An elephant can curl its trunk even though there are no bones inside it.","9-11":"The trunk combines an elephant’s nose and upper lip and is moved by many muscles.","12-14":"An elephant’s trunk acts as a muscular hydrostat, combining strength and fine control without an internal bony skeleton."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-elephants-drink-through-their-mouths","title":"Elephants drink through their mouths","question":"Tell me about Elephants drink through their mouths","words":"Elephants drink through their mouths animals","levels":{"6-8":"An elephant uses its trunk to collect water, then pours it into its mouth.","9-11":"The trunk works as a water carrier during drinking, rather than a drinking straw straight into the stomach.","12-14":"Water gathered in the trunk is transferred to the mouth for swallowing; the trunk also serves important breathing and sensory functions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-black-rhinos-browse-leaves","title":"Black rhinos browse leaves","question":"Tell me about Black rhinos browse leaves","words":"Black rhinos browse leaves animals","levels":{"6-8":"A black rhino’s pointed lip helps it pick leaves.","9-11":"Black rhinos use a grasping upper lip to select leaves and shoots from shrubs.","12-14":"The prehensile upper lip suits browsing, while mouth shape alone cannot describe every food an individual rhino may eat."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-giraffes-reach-high-branches","title":"Giraffes reach high branches","question":"Tell me about Giraffes reach high branches","words":"Giraffes reach high branches animals","levels":{"6-8":"A giraffe’s long neck helps it reach leaves high in trees.","9-11":"Giraffes usually have seven neck vertebrae, like humans, but each vertebra is much longer.","12-14":"Giraffe neck length comes mainly from elongated cervical vertebrae, illustrating how similar skeletal plans can produce different body forms."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-zebras-have-individual-patterns","title":"Zebras have individual patterns","question":"Tell me about Zebras have individual patterns","words":"Zebras have individual patterns animals","levels":{"6-8":"A zebra’s stripes are not exactly the same as another zebra’s.","9-11":"Stripe patterns vary among zebras, and researchers can use these differences to recognise individuals.","12-14":"Individual coat markings support photographic identification, though scientists still investigate the multiple functions of zebra stripes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-lions-live-in-social-groups","title":"Lions live in social groups","question":"Tell me about Lions live in social groups","words":"Lions live in social groups animals","levels":{"6-8":"Many lions live together in groups called prides.","9-11":"A pride usually includes related females and their young, with adult males associated with the group.","12-14":"Lion social organisation differs from the mostly solitary lifestyle of many other cats and varies with ecological conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-bats-are-flying-mammals","title":"Bats are flying mammals","question":"Tell me about Bats are flying mammals","words":"Bats are flying mammals animals","levels":{"6-8":"Bats are mammals that can truly fly.","9-11":"A bat’s wing is stretched skin supported by long finger bones.","12-14":"Bat wings are modified mammalian forelimbs; powered flight distinguishes them from mammals that only glide."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-penguins-are-birds-that-swim","title":"Penguins are birds that swim","question":"Tell me about Penguins are birds that swim","words":"Penguins are birds that swim animals","levels":{"6-8":"Penguins are birds even though they do not fly through the air.","9-11":"Penguin wings are adapted as flippers that help propel them underwater.","12-14":"Penguins retain feathers and other bird features, while their forelimbs are specialised for underwater locomotion rather than aerial flight."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-frogs-change-as-they-grow","title":"Frogs change as they grow","question":"Tell me about Frogs change as they grow","words":"Frogs change as they grow animals","levels":{"6-8":"Many frogs start life as tadpoles in water.","9-11":"During metamorphosis, a typical tadpole grows legs and changes from a swimming larva into a frog.","12-14":"Frog development varies among species, but metamorphosis often reorganises feeding, breathing and movement as habitat use changes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-butterflies-have-a-life-cycle","title":"Butterflies have a life cycle","question":"Tell me about Butterflies have a life cycle","words":"Butterflies have a life cycle animals","levels":{"6-8":"A butterfly begins as an egg, then becomes a caterpillar.","9-11":"A butterfly develops through egg, larva, pupa and adult stages, with major body changes during pupation.","12-14":"Complete metamorphosis separates larval growth and adult reproduction into distinct forms that can use different food resources."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-insects-have-six-legs","title":"Insects have six legs","question":"Tell me about Insects have six legs","words":"Insects have six legs animals","levels":{"6-8":"An insect has six legs attached to the middle part of its body.","9-11":"Adult insects have a head, thorax and abdomen, with three pairs of legs on the thorax.","12-14":"The insect body plan helps distinguish them from other arthropods, though immature stages and specialised species can look very different."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-spiders-are-not-insects","title":"Spiders are not insects","question":"Tell me about Spiders are not insects","words":"Spiders are not insects animals","levels":{"6-8":"A spider has eight legs, so it is not an insect.","9-11":"Spiders are arachnids, and most have two main body regions rather than an insect’s three.","12-14":"Arachnids and insects share arthropod ancestry but have different body organisation and appendage patterns."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-earthworms-help-mix-soil","title":"Earthworms help mix soil","question":"Tell me about Earthworms help mix soil","words":"Earthworms help mix soil animals","levels":{"6-8":"Earthworms move through soil and leave small tunnels.","9-11":"By burrowing and processing organic material, earthworms can affect soil structure and nutrient cycling.","12-14":"Earthworm effects depend on ecosystem context: they can improve some cultivated soils but disrupt habitats where introduced species are invasive."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-beavers-change-their-habitat","title":"Beavers change their habitat","question":"Tell me about Beavers change their habitat","words":"Beavers change their habitat animals","levels":{"6-8":"A beaver can build a dam that slows moving water.","9-11":"Beaver dams can create ponds and wetlands that alter habitat for many other organisms.","12-14":"Beavers are ecosystem engineers because their structures modify water flow, sediment storage and ecological conditions beyond the animals themselves."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-camels-store-fat-in-humps","title":"Camels store fat in humps","question":"Tell me about Camels store fat in humps","words":"Camels store fat in humps animals","levels":{"6-8":"A camel’s hump stores fat, not a tank of drinking water.","9-11":"Stored fat provides an energy reserve, while other adaptations help camels cope with dry conditions.","12-14":"Camel humps are adipose reserves; water balance depends on physiology and behaviour rather than a liquid-water reservoir in the hump."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-tortoise-carries-a-skeleton","title":"A tortoise carries a skeleton","question":"Tell me about A tortoise carries a skeleton","words":"A tortoise carries a skeleton animals","levels":{"6-8":"A tortoise’s shell is part of its body, not a removable house.","9-11":"The shell includes living bone connected to the tortoise’s skeleton and is covered by protective material.","12-14":"A turtle or tortoise shell incorporates modified ribs and vertebrae, so removing it would not leave an unharmed animal underneath."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-bird-beaks-match-different-jobs","title":"Bird beaks match different jobs","question":"Tell me about Bird beaks match different jobs","words":"Bird beaks match different jobs animals","levels":{"6-8":"A bird’s beak can help it pick, crack, probe or catch food.","9-11":"Different beak shapes often suit different feeding methods, such as cracking seeds or probing flowers.","12-14":"Beak form reflects evolutionary history and several functions; diet is important but is not the only influence on its shape."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-feathers-do-more-than-fly","title":"Feathers do more than fly","question":"Tell me about Feathers do more than fly","words":"Feathers do more than fly animals","levels":{"6-8":"Feathers can keep birds warm as well as help them fly.","9-11":"Different feather types provide insulation, protection, display and control of airflow.","12-14":"Feather structures have diverse functions, and flightless birds demonstrate that feathers are not defined solely by aerial flight."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-animals-use-camouflage","title":"Animals use camouflage","question":"Tell me about Animals use camouflage","words":"Animals use camouflage animals","levels":{"6-8":"An animal’s colours can help it blend into its surroundings.","9-11":"Camouflage can make an animal harder for predators or prey to detect.","12-14":"Concealment depends on background, lighting, behaviour and the observer’s visual system; a pattern is not equally effective everywhere."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-food-webs-link-many-lives","title":"Food webs link many lives","question":"Tell me about Food webs link many lives","words":"Food webs link many lives animals","levels":{"6-8":"One animal can be connected to many others through food.","9-11":"A food web shows overlapping feeding relationships rather than just one straight food chain.","12-14":"Food webs represent multiple pathways of energy transfer, and changes in one population can influence others through direct or indirect links."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-leaves-make-food-from-light","title":"Leaves make food from light","question":"Tell me about Leaves make food from light","words":"Leaves make food from light science","levels":{"6-8":"Green leaves can use sunlight to help make food.","9-11":"Photosynthesis uses light energy to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugars.","12-14":"Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy; mineral nutrients from soil are necessary but are not the plant’s main carbon source."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-roots-take-up-water","title":"Roots take up water","question":"Tell me about Roots take up water","words":"Roots take up water science","levels":{"6-8":"Roots help a plant take water from the ground.","9-11":"Fine roots and root hairs increase contact with soil, helping absorb water and dissolved nutrients.","12-14":"Water uptake depends on roots, soil conditions and water-potential differences, with transport linked to the plant’s wider water system."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-stems-are-transport-routes","title":"Stems are transport routes","question":"Tell me about Stems are transport routes","words":"Stems are transport routes science","levels":{"6-8":"A stem holds up leaves and carries materials through a plant.","9-11":"Special tissues transport water and minerals upward and move sugars between parts of the plant.","12-14":"Xylem and phloem perform different transport roles, connecting sources and sinks rather than acting as one undifferentiated pipe."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-flowers-help-plants-reproduce","title":"Flowers help plants reproduce","question":"Tell me about Flowers help plants reproduce","words":"Flowers help plants reproduce science","levels":{"6-8":"Many flowers help a plant make seeds.","9-11":"Flowers contain reproductive structures, and pollination moves pollen to a suitable receiving part.","12-14":"Flowering-plant reproduction involves pollination and fertilisation, which are related processes but are not the same event."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-seeds-hold-young-plants","title":"Seeds hold young plants","question":"Tell me about Seeds hold young plants","words":"Seeds hold young plants science","levels":{"6-8":"A seed can contain a tiny young plant.","9-11":"A seed protects an embryo and often includes stored food to support early growth.","12-14":"Seed structure combines an embryo, protective tissues and nutrient reserves that vary among plant groups and species."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-germination-needs-suitable-conditions","title":"Germination needs suitable conditions","question":"Tell me about Germination needs suitable conditions","words":"Germination needs suitable conditions science","levels":{"6-8":"A seed starts growing when its needs are met.","9-11":"Many seeds need water, oxygen and suitable temperatures to germinate.","12-14":"Dormancy and germination requirements vary: some seeds also need particular light, temperature changes or other environmental cues."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-some-seeds-ride-the-wind","title":"Some seeds ride the wind","question":"Tell me about Some seeds ride the wind","words":"Some seeds ride the wind science","levels":{"6-8":"Some seeds have wings or fluffy parts that help the wind carry them.","9-11":"Wind dispersal can move seeds away from the parent plant into new growing places.","12-14":"Dispersal structures affect air resistance and movement, but successful transport does not guarantee a suitable site for establishment."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-animals-spread-seeds","title":"Animals spread seeds","question":"Tell me about Animals spread seeds","words":"Animals spread seeds science","levels":{"6-8":"Animals can carry seeds to new places.","9-11":"Seeds may cling to fur or travel after an animal eats a fruit and later passes the seeds.","12-14":"Animal-mediated dispersal includes external attachment and ingestion, linking plant reproduction to animal movement and behaviour."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fruits-protect-seeds","title":"Fruits protect seeds","question":"Tell me about Fruits protect seeds","words":"Fruits protect seeds science","levels":{"6-8":"Many fruits grow around seeds after a flower is fertilised.","9-11":"Botanically, a fruit develops from a flower’s ovary, so some foods called vegetables are fruits.","12-14":"Culinary and botanical categories use different criteria: tomatoes and cucumbers are fruits in a botanical classification."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-potatoes-are-underground-stems","title":"Potatoes are underground stems","question":"Tell me about Potatoes are underground stems","words":"Potatoes are underground stems science","levels":{"6-8":"A potato is a swollen underground stem, not a root.","9-11":"The small eyes on a potato are buds that can grow into new shoots.","12-14":"Potato tubers are modified stems used for storage and vegetative reproduction, unlike root tubers such as sweet potatoes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-carrots-store-food-in-roots","title":"Carrots store food in roots","question":"Tell me about Carrots store food in roots","words":"Carrots store food in roots science","levels":{"6-8":"The part of a carrot we usually eat is a thick root.","9-11":"Carrots store reserves in an enlarged taproot, which supports the plant’s growth.","12-14":"A carrot’s storage root contains tissues that accumulate reserves; edible plant organs need not all be fruits or stems."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-bamboo-is-a-grass","title":"Bamboo is a grass","question":"Tell me about Bamboo is a grass","words":"Bamboo is a grass science","levels":{"6-8":"Tall bamboo is a kind of grass, even when it looks like a tree.","9-11":"Bamboos belong to the grass family and have jointed stems called culms.","12-14":"Bamboo illustrates that tree-like size and growth form do not determine botanical family membership."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-cacti-save-water","title":"Cacti save water","question":"Tell me about Cacti save water","words":"Cacti save water science","levels":{"6-8":"Many cacti have thick stems that store water.","9-11":"Spines, water-storing tissues and other adaptations help many cacti survive dry habitats.","12-14":"Many cacti combine succulent stems with water-conserving physiology; adaptations differ across species and do not make them independent of water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-plants-release-water-vapour","title":"Plants release water vapour","question":"Tell me about Plants release water vapour","words":"Plants release water vapour science","levels":{"6-8":"Leaves can release water into the air.","9-11":"Water evaporating from plant surfaces, especially leaves, is called transpiration.","12-14":"Transpiration links plants to the water cycle and can help drive xylem flow, while stomata regulate gas exchange and water loss."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-leaves-have-tiny-openings","title":"Leaves have tiny openings","question":"Tell me about Leaves have tiny openings","words":"Leaves have tiny openings science","levels":{"6-8":"Many leaves have tiny openings that let gases move in and out.","9-11":"Stomata help leaves take in carbon dioxide and release water vapour.","12-14":"Guard cells alter stomatal openings, balancing carbon acquisition with water conservation as environmental conditions change."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fungi-are-not-plants","title":"Fungi are not plants","question":"Tell me about Fungi are not plants","words":"Fungi are not plants science","levels":{"6-8":"A mushroom is part of a fungus, not a plant.","9-11":"Fungi belong to their own biological kingdom and obtain nutrients differently from green plants.","12-14":"Fungi usually digest organic material externally and absorb nutrients; they do not photosynthesise in the way green plants do."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-mushroom-is-only-part","title":"A mushroom is only part","question":"Tell me about A mushroom is only part","words":"A mushroom is only part science","levels":{"6-8":"The mushroom you see can be just a small part of a fungus.","9-11":"Many fungi grow as networks of fine threads called hyphae, which form a mycelium.","12-14":"A mushroom is a reproductive structure in some fungi; the nutrient-absorbing mycelium may extend through a much larger substrate."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-roots-can-partner-with-fungi","title":"Roots can partner with fungi","question":"Tell me about Roots can partner with fungi","words":"Roots can partner with fungi science","levels":{"6-8":"Some fungi help plant roots gather nutrients.","9-11":"In mycorrhizal partnerships, fungi can supply nutrients while the plant supplies sugars.","12-14":"Mycorrhizal associations exchange resources between partners; their benefits and costs depend on species and environmental conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-decomposers-recycle-materials","title":"Decomposers recycle materials","question":"Tell me about Decomposers recycle materials","words":"Decomposers recycle materials science","levels":{"6-8":"Fungi and other decomposers help break down dead things.","9-11":"Decomposition returns nutrients from dead material to the environment, where organisms can use them again.","12-14":"Decomposers transform organic matter and release nutrients, connecting biological activity to wider carbon and nutrient cycles."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-pollen-travels-in-different-ways","title":"Pollen travels in different ways","question":"Tell me about Pollen travels in different ways","words":"Pollen travels in different ways science","levels":{"6-8":"Pollen can move on animals or on the wind.","9-11":"Some flowers attract pollinating animals, while others release pollen that the wind carries.","12-14":"Pollination strategies influence floral traits and pollen production; brightly coloured petals are not necessary for every flowering plant."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-your-body-is-made-of-cells","title":"Your body is made of cells","question":"Tell me about Your body is made of cells","words":"Your body is made of cells science","levels":{"6-8":"Your body is built from tiny living units called cells.","9-11":"Different cell types perform different jobs, such as carrying oxygen or sending signals.","12-14":"Multicellular bodies rely on specialised cells working together in tissues and organs, rather than every cell performing every function."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-bones-are-living-tissue","title":"Bones are living tissue","question":"Tell me about Bones are living tissue","words":"Bones are living tissue science","levels":{"6-8":"Your bones are living parts of your body.","9-11":"Bone contains cells and blood vessels and is continually maintained and remodelled.","12-14":"Bone is a dynamic tissue whose cells coordinate formation and breakdown, adapting its structure over time."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-muscles-pull-to-move","title":"Muscles pull to move","question":"Tell me about Muscles pull to move","words":"Muscles pull to move science","levels":{"6-8":"Muscles help move your bones by pulling on them.","9-11":"Skeletal muscles contract and usually work in groups around joints.","12-14":"Muscles generate tension rather than pushing bones directly; coordinated opposing groups allow controlled movement in different directions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-tendons-connect-muscle-and-bone","title":"Tendons connect muscle and bone","question":"Tell me about Tendons connect muscle and bone","words":"Tendons connect muscle and bone science","levels":{"6-8":"A tendon helps connect a muscle to a bone.","9-11":"Tendons transfer force from contracting muscles to the skeleton.","12-14":"Tendon connective tissue transmits tensile forces, helping convert muscle activity into movement or stabilisation of joints."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-joints-allow-different-movements","title":"Joints allow different movements","question":"Tell me about Joints allow different movements","words":"Joints allow different movements science","levels":{"6-8":"A joint is a place where bones meet.","9-11":"Different joints allow different movements, such as bending at an elbow or rotating at a shoulder.","12-14":"Joint structure constrains movement and balances mobility with stability; not all joints permit free motion."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-skin-is-an-organ","title":"Skin is an organ","question":"Tell me about Skin is an organ","words":"Skin is an organ science","levels":{"6-8":"Your skin is an organ covering and protecting your body.","9-11":"Skin helps form a protective barrier and contains structures involved in sensation and temperature control.","12-14":"Skin includes multiple layers and specialised cells; its barrier function, sensory roles and regulation involve several interacting systems."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-heart-is-a-pump","title":"The heart is a pump","question":"Tell me about The heart is a pump","words":"The heart is a pump science","levels":{"6-8":"Your heart pumps blood around your body.","9-11":"The heart’s muscular walls contract to move blood through connected blood vessels.","12-14":"Coordinated heart chambers and valves maintain circulation, with separate pathways serving the lungs and the rest of the body."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-blood-carries-materials","title":"Blood carries materials","question":"Tell me about Blood carries materials","words":"Blood carries materials science","levels":{"6-8":"Blood moves useful materials from place to place inside you.","9-11":"Blood carries oxygen, nutrients and other substances, while also transporting waste products.","12-14":"Blood combines cells and plasma to support transport, defence and regulation; different components perform different roles."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-red-blood-cells-carry-oxygen","title":"Red blood cells carry oxygen","question":"Tell me about Red blood cells carry oxygen","words":"Red blood cells carry oxygen science","levels":{"6-8":"Red blood cells help carry oxygen around your body.","9-11":"A protein called haemoglobin in red blood cells binds oxygen for transport.","12-14":"Haemoglobin’s reversible oxygen binding supports delivery from lungs to tissues, while oxygen availability influences how much is released."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-lungs-exchange-gases","title":"Lungs exchange gases","question":"Tell me about Lungs exchange gases","words":"Lungs exchange gases science","levels":{"6-8":"Your lungs help bring oxygen into your body and send carbon dioxide out.","9-11":"Tiny air sacs in the lungs provide surfaces where gases move between air and blood.","12-14":"Alveoli provide a large exchange area across thin barriers; ventilation and blood flow both contribute to effective gas exchange."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-your-diaphragm-helps-breathing","title":"Your diaphragm helps breathing","question":"Tell me about Your diaphragm helps breathing","words":"Your diaphragm helps breathing science","levels":{"6-8":"A large muscle below your lungs helps you breathe.","9-11":"When the diaphragm contracts, it helps enlarge the chest cavity so air moves into the lungs.","12-14":"Diaphragm movement changes thoracic volume and pressure; air flows because of the resulting pressure difference rather than being pulled by lung muscles."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-digestion-changes-food","title":"Digestion changes food","question":"Tell me about Digestion changes food","words":"Digestion changes food science","levels":{"6-8":"Digestion breaks food into smaller parts your body can use.","9-11":"Mechanical movement and digestive chemicals help convert food into absorbable substances.","12-14":"Digestion combines physical processing with enzyme-mediated reactions, while absorption moves resulting molecules across the digestive lining."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-teeth-have-different-shapes","title":"Teeth have different shapes","question":"Tell me about Teeth have different shapes","words":"Teeth have different shapes science","levels":{"6-8":"Your front and back teeth have different jobs.","9-11":"Incisors help cut food, while broad molars help crush and grind it.","12-14":"Human tooth shape reflects different mechanical roles, with enamel providing a hard outer surface over underlying tissues."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-saliva-starts-work-early","title":"Saliva starts work early","question":"Tell me about Saliva starts work early","words":"Saliva starts work early science","levels":{"6-8":"Your mouth adds saliva while you chew food.","9-11":"Saliva moistens food and contains an enzyme that begins breaking down some starch.","12-14":"Digestion begins before food reaches the stomach; salivary amylase acts on starch under suitable conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-brain-receives-messages","title":"The brain receives messages","question":"Tell me about The brain receives messages","words":"The brain receives messages science","levels":{"6-8":"Your brain receives information from many parts of your body.","9-11":"Nerves carry signals between the brain, spinal cord and the rest of the body.","12-14":"The nervous system integrates sensory input and coordinates responses through electrical and chemical signalling."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-eyes-detect-light","title":"Eyes detect light","question":"Tell me about Eyes detect light","words":"Eyes detect light science","levels":{"6-8":"Your eyes need light to see things.","9-11":"Light-sensitive cells in the retina convert incoming light into signals sent toward the brain.","12-14":"Vision depends on both optical focusing and neural processing; the brain interprets signals rather than receiving a finished photograph."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ears-detect-vibrations","title":"Ears detect vibrations","question":"Tell me about Ears detect vibrations","words":"Ears detect vibrations science","levels":{"6-8":"Sounds make parts inside your ears vibrate.","9-11":"The ear changes sound vibrations into signals the nervous system can interpret.","12-14":"Mechanical vibrations ultimately stimulate specialised sensory cells, connecting pressure waves outside the body to neural signals."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-smell-begins-with-molecules","title":"Smell begins with molecules","question":"Tell me about Smell begins with molecules","words":"Smell begins with molecules science","levels":{"6-8":"Tiny molecules in the air can help you smell flowers or food.","9-11":"Smell receptors respond to airborne molecules that reach the nasal cavity.","12-14":"Olfaction begins with molecular interactions at receptors and depends on neural processing to distinguish and interpret odours."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-taste-and-smell-work-together","title":"Taste and smell work together","question":"Tell me about Taste and smell work together","words":"Taste and smell work together science","levels":{"6-8":"Your nose helps food have its full flavour.","9-11":"Taste receptors and smell signals both contribute to the experience of flavour.","12-14":"Flavour is multisensory, combining gustatory, olfactory and other information rather than arising from taste buds alone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-body-has-internal-clocks","title":"The body has internal clocks","question":"Tell me about The body has internal clocks","words":"The body has internal clocks science","levels":{"6-8":"Your body has daily rhythms, including changes linked to sleep and waking.","9-11":"Circadian rhythms help coordinate body functions over roughly a day and respond to cues such as light.","12-14":"Biological clocks generate circadian timing, while environmental signals help align these rhythms with the external day–night cycle."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-push-can-change-motion","title":"A push can change motion","question":"Tell me about A push can change motion","words":"A push can change motion science","levels":{"6-8":"A push or pull can change how something moves.","9-11":"A force can change an object’s speed or direction, depending on the other forces acting on it.","12-14":"Acceleration depends on net force and mass; balanced forces can act on an object without changing its velocity."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-friction-resists-sliding","title":"Friction resists sliding","question":"Tell me about Friction resists sliding","words":"Friction resists sliding science","levels":{"6-8":"Friction makes it harder for two touching surfaces to slide.","9-11":"Friction can slow sliding objects and also helps shoes grip the ground.","12-14":"Friction is not simply an unwanted loss: static friction enables traction, while sliding friction converts mechanical energy into internal energy."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-gravity-reaches-across-space","title":"Gravity reaches across space","question":"Tell me about Gravity reaches across space","words":"Gravity reaches across space science","levels":{"6-8":"Gravity pulls objects with mass toward one another.","9-11":"Earth’s gravity keeps us near the ground and helps keep the Moon in orbit.","12-14":"Gravitation acts between masses, and orbital motion combines gravitational acceleration with an object’s existing sideways velocity."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mass-and-weight-differ","title":"Mass and weight differ","question":"Tell me about Mass and weight differ","words":"Mass and weight differ science","levels":{"6-8":"Mass tells how much matter something has; weight depends on gravity.","9-11":"An object keeps the same mass on the Moon, but its weight is smaller there.","12-14":"Weight is gravitational force, while mass measures inertia; changing gravitational field strength changes weight without changing an object’s mass."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-ramp-spreads-out-a-lift","title":"A ramp spreads out a lift","question":"Tell me about A ramp spreads out a lift","words":"A ramp spreads out a lift science","levels":{"6-8":"A ramp can make raising an object easier over a longer path.","9-11":"An inclined plane can reduce the force needed by increasing the distance over which the force acts.","12-14":"An ideal ramp trades force for distance rather than creating energy; real ramps also involve frictional losses."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-levers-trade-force-and-distance","title":"Levers trade force and distance","question":"Tell me about Levers trade force and distance","words":"Levers trade force and distance science","levels":{"6-8":"A long handle can help you lift or turn something.","9-11":"A lever pivots around a fulcrum, and the distances from that pivot affect its mechanical advantage.","12-14":"Turning effects depend on force and perpendicular distance from the pivot, so changing lever geometry changes the force required."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-gears-can-change-speed","title":"Gears can change speed","question":"Tell me about Gears can change speed","words":"Gears can change speed science","levels":{"6-8":"Turning one gear can make another gear turn.","9-11":"Interlocking gears transfer rotation, and differently sized gears can change rotational speed.","12-14":"Gear ratios trade angular speed against torque in an ideal system, while meshing external gears rotate in opposite directions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-springs-can-store-energy","title":"Springs can store energy","question":"Tell me about Springs can store energy","words":"Springs can store energy science","levels":{"6-8":"A stretched or squeezed spring can push or pull back.","9-11":"Deforming a spring can store elastic potential energy that is released when it returns.","12-14":"Within suitable limits, elastic deformation stores recoverable energy; excessive deformation can permanently change or damage a spring."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sound-needs-material","title":"Sound needs material","question":"Tell me about Sound needs material","words":"Sound needs material science","levels":{"6-8":"Sound can travel through air, water or solid objects.","9-11":"Sound is a vibration that moves through a medium, so it cannot travel through an ideal vacuum.","12-14":"Mechanical sound waves require interacting particles, unlike electromagnetic waves that can propagate through empty space."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-pitch-follows-vibration-rate","title":"Pitch follows vibration rate","question":"Tell me about Pitch follows vibration rate","words":"Pitch follows vibration rate science","levels":{"6-8":"A faster vibration can make a higher-pitched sound.","9-11":"The frequency of a sound wave is linked to its pitch, while amplitude is linked to loudness.","12-14":"Frequency and amplitude describe different wave properties; perceived pitch and loudness also depend on human hearing and context."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-echoes-are-returning-sounds","title":"Echoes are returning sounds","question":"Tell me about Echoes are returning sounds","words":"Echoes are returning sounds science","levels":{"6-8":"An echo is sound that bounces back to you.","9-11":"Sound reflected by a sufficiently distant surface can reach you as a separate echo.","12-14":"Reflection and travel time create echoes; closely spaced reflections may instead blend into reverberation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-light-can-reflect","title":"Light can reflect","question":"Tell me about Light can reflect","words":"Light can reflect science","levels":{"6-8":"A mirror sends light back in a new direction.","9-11":"On a smooth mirror, the angle of reflection matches the angle of incidence.","12-14":"Specular reflection produces orderly directions from a smooth surface, whereas rough surfaces scatter reflected light over many directions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-light-bends-between-materials","title":"Light bends between materials","question":"Tell me about Light bends between materials","words":"Light bends between materials science","levels":{"6-8":"A straw in water can look bent even when it is straight.","9-11":"Light changes direction when it passes at an angle between materials where its speed differs.","12-14":"Refraction explains apparent displacement at an interface; the object itself need not change shape for its image to shift."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-white-light-has-many-colours","title":"White light has many colours","question":"Tell me about White light has many colours","words":"White light has many colours science","levels":{"6-8":"White light can be separated into different colours.","9-11":"A prism can spread white light because different wavelengths bend by different amounts.","12-14":"Dispersion separates wavelengths through wavelength-dependent refraction, showing that white light is not a single spectral colour."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-shadow-blocks-light","title":"A shadow blocks light","question":"Tell me about A shadow blocks light","words":"A shadow blocks light science","levels":{"6-8":"A shadow forms where an object blocks light from a source.","9-11":"The size and sharpness of a shadow depend on the light source and the positions of objects.","12-14":"Extended light sources produce partial and full shadow regions, so not every shadow has a perfectly sharp edge."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-transparent-things-transmit-light","title":"Transparent things transmit light","question":"Tell me about Transparent things transmit light","words":"Transparent things transmit light science","levels":{"6-8":"Clear glass lets much visible light pass through it.","9-11":"Materials differ in how much light they transmit, absorb and reflect.","12-14":"Transparency depends on wavelength and material structure; a material transparent to visible light may block other electromagnetic radiation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-magnets-have-two-poles","title":"Magnets have two poles","question":"Tell me about Magnets have two poles","words":"Magnets have two poles science","levels":{"6-8":"A magnet has a north pole and a south pole.","9-11":"Like magnetic poles repel, while unlike poles attract.","12-14":"Cutting an ordinary magnet produces smaller magnets with both poles, rather than separating isolated north and south poles."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-not-every-metal-is-magnetic","title":"Not every metal is magnetic","question":"Tell me about Not every metal is magnetic","words":"Not every metal is magnetic science","levels":{"6-8":"A magnet does not strongly attract every kind of metal.","9-11":"Iron and some alloys are strongly attracted to magnets, but many common metals are not.","12-14":"Magnetic behaviour depends on electronic structure and organisation, so metallic conductivity does not imply ferromagnetism."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-heat-moves-from-warmer-to-cooler","title":"Heat moves from warmer to cooler","question":"Tell me about Heat moves from warmer to cooler","words":"Heat moves from warmer to cooler science","levels":{"6-8":"A warm object can pass energy to a cooler object.","9-11":"Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.","12-14":"Thermal energy can transfer by conduction, convection and radiation, with net transfer from higher to lower temperature in ordinary conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-floating-depends-on-density","title":"Floating depends on density","question":"Tell me about Floating depends on density","words":"Floating depends on density science","levels":{"6-8":"A large ship can float even though it is heavy.","9-11":"An object floats when the buoyant force can balance its weight, which depends on water displaced.","12-14":"Average density and displaced-fluid weight matter; a hollow steel ship can float although solid steel is denser than water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ice-is-less-dense-than-liquid-water","title":"Ice is less dense than liquid water","question":"Tell me about Ice is less dense than liquid water","words":"Ice is less dense than liquid water science","levels":{"6-8":"Ice usually floats on liquid water.","9-11":"Water expands when it freezes under ordinary conditions, making ice less dense than the liquid.","12-14":"The open crystal structure of ordinary ice lowers its density, so floating ice can insulate liquid water beneath it."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-melting-changes-a-state","title":"Melting changes a state","question":"Tell me about Melting changes a state","words":"Melting changes a state science","levels":{"6-8":"An ice cube can become liquid water without becoming a different substance.","9-11":"Melting changes a solid into a liquid; the material’s chemical identity can remain the same.","12-14":"A phase transition changes physical organisation rather than necessarily changing chemical composition, as in ice melting to liquid water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-water-vapour-is-invisible","title":"Water vapour is invisible","question":"Tell me about Water vapour is invisible","words":"Water vapour is invisible science","levels":{"6-8":"Water in its gas form cannot be seen with your eyes.","9-11":"The white cloud near a kettle contains tiny liquid droplets, not visible water vapour itself.","12-14":"Condensation makes gaseous water visible indirectly by producing droplets that scatter light; the vapour phase is normally invisible."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-dissolved-salt-has-not-vanished","title":"Dissolved salt has not vanished","question":"Tell me about Dissolved salt has not vanished","words":"Dissolved salt has not vanished science","levels":{"6-8":"Salt can disappear from sight when it dissolves in water.","9-11":"Dissolved salt is spread through the water as particles too small to see individually.","12-14":"Dissolution disperses salt’s ions through water; loss of visible crystals does not mean the material has ceased to exist."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mixtures-can-keep-their-ingredients","title":"Mixtures can keep their ingredients","question":"Tell me about Mixtures can keep their ingredients","words":"Mixtures can keep their ingredients science","levels":{"6-8":"A mixture can contain several substances together.","9-11":"Sand mixed with water remains a mixture whose ingredients can often be separated physically.","12-14":"Mixtures have variable composition and retain constituent substances, unlike a pure compound with a defined chemical composition."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-filtering-sorts-by-size","title":"Filtering sorts by size","question":"Tell me about Filtering sorts by size","words":"Filtering sorts by size science","levels":{"6-8":"A filter can catch particles while letting a liquid pass.","9-11":"Filters have openings that allow some materials through while holding back larger particles.","12-14":"Ordinary filtration separates suspended particles by size and other interactions; it does not automatically remove dissolved substances or all microbes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-evaporation-can-leave-crystals","title":"Evaporation can leave crystals","question":"Tell me about Evaporation can leave crystals","words":"Evaporation can leave crystals science","levels":{"6-8":"When salty water dries, solid salt can be left behind.","9-11":"Water can evaporate while dissolved salt remains, allowing crystals to form.","12-14":"Solvent loss can produce a supersaturated solution and crystallisation, separating a dissolved solid without making the salt evaporate with the water."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-atoms-build-ordinary-matter","title":"Atoms build ordinary matter","question":"Tell me about Atoms build ordinary matter","words":"Atoms build ordinary matter science","levels":{"6-8":"Tiny units called atoms help make the things around you.","9-11":"Different chemical elements are defined by different kinds of atoms.","12-14":"An element’s identity is set by proton number; atomic arrangement and bonding help determine the properties of materials."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-molecules-join-atoms","title":"Molecules join atoms","question":"Tell me about Molecules join atoms","words":"Molecules join atoms science","levels":{"6-8":"A molecule contains atoms joined together.","9-11":"A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.","12-14":"Molecular properties depend on composition and structure; a compound’s behaviour can differ greatly from that of its constituent elements."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-air-is-a-mixture","title":"Air is a mixture","question":"Tell me about Air is a mixture","words":"Air is a mixture science","levels":{"6-8":"Air contains several gases mixed together.","9-11":"Most dry air near Earth’s surface is nitrogen and oxygen, with smaller amounts of other gases.","12-14":"Atmospheric composition includes trace gases and variable water vapour; oxygen is important but is not the largest component of dry air."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rust-is-a-chemical-change","title":"Rust is a chemical change","question":"Tell me about Rust is a chemical change","words":"Rust is a chemical change science","levels":{"6-8":"Iron can react and form rust.","9-11":"Rusting involves iron reacting with oxygen and water, producing iron-containing corrosion products.","12-14":"Corrosion changes chemical composition and depends on environmental conditions; rust is not merely red paint on unchanged iron."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-copper-conducts-electricity","title":"Copper conducts electricity","question":"Tell me about Copper conducts electricity","words":"Copper conducts electricity science","levels":{"6-8":"Copper lets electric charge move through it easily.","9-11":"Copper is widely used in electrical wiring because it has low electrical resistance.","12-14":"Mobile electrons in metallic copper support conduction; wire size, temperature and length still affect its resistance."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rubber-can-act-as-an-insulator","title":"Rubber can act as an insulator","question":"Tell me about Rubber can act as an insulator","words":"Rubber can act as an insulator science","levels":{"6-8":"Some materials make it hard for electric charge to move through them.","9-11":"Rubber and many plastics are electrical insulators under suitable conditions.","12-14":"Insulation is a material property with limits: voltage, damage, moisture and composition can affect whether a material remains protective."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-glass-and-crystals-differ","title":"Glass and crystals differ","question":"Tell me about Glass and crystals differ","words":"Glass and crystals differ science","levels":{"6-8":"Ordinary window glass is not arranged like a neat crystal.","9-11":"Many solids have ordered crystal structures, but ordinary glass has a disordered structure.","12-14":"Glass is an amorphous solid: it lacks the long-range repeating atomic arrangement characteristic of a crystal."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-materials-expand-when-heated","title":"Materials expand when heated","question":"Tell me about Materials expand when heated","words":"Materials expand when heated science","levels":{"6-8":"Many materials get slightly larger when warmed.","9-11":"Heating usually increases particle motion and can increase the average spacing between particles.","12-14":"Thermal expansion depends on structure and temperature; water near freezing is a notable exception to a simple universal expansion rule."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-density-compares-mass-and-volume","title":"Density compares mass and volume","question":"Tell me about Density compares mass and volume","words":"Density compares mass and volume science","levels":{"6-8":"Two objects of the same size can have different masses.","9-11":"Density tells how much mass is contained in a given volume.","12-14":"Density is mass divided by volume, so neither size nor heaviness alone is enough to compare materials accurately."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-paper-comes-from-fibres","title":"Paper comes from fibres","question":"Tell me about Paper comes from fibres","words":"Paper comes from fibres science","levels":{"6-8":"Paper is made from small fibres pressed together.","9-11":"Many papers use cellulose fibres from plants, arranged into a thin sheet.","12-14":"Paper properties depend on fibre source, processing, additives and sheet structure, not simply on the tree species alone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-wood-has-a-grain","title":"Wood has a grain","question":"Tell me about Wood has a grain","words":"Wood has a grain science","levels":{"6-8":"Wood often shows lines because of how a tree grew.","9-11":"Wood grain reflects the arrangement of cells and fibres formed during growth.","12-14":"Wood is an anisotropic biological material, so strength and other properties can differ along and across its grain."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ceramics-begin-with-shaping-and-firing","title":"Ceramics begin with shaping and firing","question":"Tell me about Ceramics begin with shaping and firing","words":"Ceramics begin with shaping and firing science","levels":{"6-8":"Many ceramic objects start as shaped clay.","9-11":"Heating shaped clay changes it into a harder ceramic material.","12-14":"Firing produces physical and chemical changes that make many ceramics durable and heat-resistant, though their properties vary with composition and processing."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-recycling-changes-material-use","title":"Recycling changes material use","question":"Tell me about Recycling changes material use","words":"Recycling changes material use science","levels":{"6-8":"Recycling can turn some discarded materials into useful materials again.","9-11":"Paper, metals, glass and some plastics can be processed for reuse, depending on local systems.","12-14":"Recyclability depends on material type, contamination, collection and processing infrastructure; a recycling symbol alone does not guarantee local acceptance."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-zero-has-an-important-job","title":"Zero has an important job","question":"Tell me about Zero has an important job","words":"Zero has an important job math","levels":{"6-8":"Zero can show that there are none of something.","9-11":"In our number system, zero also holds a place, so 205 is different from 25.","12-14":"Zero functions as a number and as a positional placeholder; its role is essential to compact decimal notation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-place-value-changes-a-digit","title":"Place value changes a digit","question":"Tell me about Place value changes a digit","words":"Place value changes a digit math","levels":{"6-8":"The same digit can mean different amounts in different places.","9-11":"In 222, the digits represent two hundreds, two tens and two ones.","12-14":"In base ten, adjacent positions differ by a factor of ten, so a digit’s value depends on its position."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-even-numbers-make-pairs","title":"Even numbers make pairs","question":"Tell me about Even numbers make pairs","words":"Even numbers make pairs math","levels":{"6-8":"An even number of objects can be split into pairs with none left.","9-11":"Even whole numbers are divisible by two without a remainder.","12-14":"Even integers are multiples of two, including zero; parity describes a structural property rather than the apparent size of a number."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-odd-plus-odd-is-even","title":"Odd plus odd is even","question":"Tell me about Odd plus odd is even","words":"Odd plus odd is even math","levels":{"6-8":"Two odd-sized groups can join to make an even-sized group.","9-11":"Each odd number leaves one object after pairing, and those two leftovers make another pair.","12-14":"Writing odd integers as 2a+1 and 2b+1 shows their sum equals 2(a+b+1), which is even."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-multiplication-can-use-an-array","title":"Multiplication can use an array","question":"Tell me about Multiplication can use an array","words":"Multiplication can use an array math","levels":{"6-8":"Rows of dots can help show multiplication.","9-11":"An array with three rows of four dots contains the same total as four rows of three.","12-14":"Rectangular arrays model commutativity for whole-number multiplication: swapping dimensions preserves the number of cells."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-division-can-mean-sharing","title":"Division can mean sharing","question":"Tell me about Division can mean sharing","words":"Division can mean sharing math","levels":{"6-8":"Division can tell how many each person gets when a group is shared equally.","9-11":"Twelve objects shared among three people gives four each, while grouping into threes asks a different division question.","12-14":"Partition and measurement division describe different situations that can have the same arithmetic expression."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fractions-need-equal-parts","title":"Fractions need equal parts","question":"Tell me about Fractions need equal parts","words":"Fractions need equal parts math","levels":{"6-8":"One half means one of two equal parts of a whole.","9-11":"Equal parts are essential: cutting something into two uneven pieces does not make each piece one half.","12-14":"A fraction describes a relationship to a specified whole; equal partition and the choice of unit determine its meaning."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-equivalent-fractions-name-the-same-amount","title":"Equivalent fractions name the same amount","question":"Tell me about Equivalent fractions name the same amount","words":"Equivalent fractions name the same amount math","levels":{"6-8":"One half and two quarters can show the same amount.","9-11":"Multiplying a fraction’s numerator and denominator by the same non-zero number keeps its value.","12-14":"Equivalent fractions preserve the ratio between numerator and denominator, allowing different representations of one rational number."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-bigger-denominator-can-mean-smaller-pieces","title":"A bigger denominator can mean smaller pieces","question":"Tell me about A bigger denominator can mean smaller pieces","words":"A bigger denominator can mean smaller pieces math","levels":{"6-8":"When the whole stays the same, eighths are smaller pieces than quarters.","9-11":"Dividing the same whole into more equal parts makes each unit fraction smaller.","12-14":"For positive unit fractions with a fixed whole, increasing the denominator decreases value; denominator size alone cannot compare arbitrary fractions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-decimals-are-another-notation","title":"Decimals are another notation","question":"Tell me about Decimals are another notation","words":"Decimals are another notation math","levels":{"6-8":"A half can also be written as 0.5.","9-11":"Decimal places represent tenths, hundredths and further powers of ten.","12-14":"Finite decimals are fractions with denominators that can divide a power of ten, connecting place value with rational-number representation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-percent-means-out-of-one-hundred","title":"Percent means out of one hundred","question":"Tell me about Percent means out of one hundred","words":"Percent means out of one hundred math","levels":{"6-8":"Fifty percent means fifty out of one hundred equal parts.","9-11":"Percentages describe proportions using a common base of one hundred.","12-14":"A percentage is a dimensionless ratio; interpreting a percent change requires knowing the original reference amount."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-negative-numbers-go-below-zero","title":"Negative numbers go below zero","question":"Tell me about Negative numbers go below zero","words":"Negative numbers go below zero math","levels":{"6-8":"Numbers can continue past zero in the opposite direction.","9-11":"Negative numbers can represent quantities such as temperatures below zero or positions on the other side of a starting point.","12-14":"Signed numbers encode direction or relation to a reference, so a negative value is not simply an absence of quantity."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-number-line-has-no-last-number","title":"A number line has no last number","question":"Tell me about A number line has no last number","words":"A number line has no last number math","levels":{"6-8":"You can always find a whole number larger than the one you chose.","9-11":"Adding one to any whole number gives another whole number, so the counting numbers have no largest member.","12-14":"The natural numbers are unbounded: assuming a greatest one leads to a larger number by adding one."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-between-fractions-there-are-more-fractions","title":"Between fractions there are more fractions","question":"Tell me about Between fractions there are more fractions","words":"Between fractions there are more fractions math","levels":{"6-8":"Two different fractions can have another fraction between them.","9-11":"Taking the average of two different fractions gives a new fraction between them.","12-14":"Rational numbers are dense: between any two distinct rational values lies another rational value, despite gaps in a drawn number line."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-prime-numbers-have-just-two-factors","title":"Prime numbers have just two factors","question":"Tell me about Prime numbers have just two factors","words":"Prime numbers have just two factors math","levels":{"6-8":"A prime number has only one and itself as positive factors.","9-11":"Two is prime, but one is not, because a prime needs exactly two different positive factors.","12-14":"Prime numbers are integers greater than one with exactly two positive divisors; two is the only even prime."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-squares-can-be-counted-in-rows","title":"Squares can be counted in rows","question":"Tell me about Squares can be counted in rows","words":"Squares can be counted in rows math","levels":{"6-8":"A square number can be arranged as a square of dots.","9-11":"Numbers such as 9 and 16 are square numbers because they equal 3×3 and 4×4.","12-14":"A perfect square is an integer multiplied by itself; square arrays provide a geometric model for this numerical property."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-patterns-need-a-rule","title":"Patterns need a rule","question":"Tell me about Patterns need a rule","words":"Patterns need a rule math","levels":{"6-8":"A list of numbers can follow a rule.","9-11":"Adding the same amount each time makes an arithmetic sequence.","12-14":"A finite list can fit many possible rules, so a proposed next term depends on the intended generating rule and context."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-estimates-have-a-purpose","title":"Estimates have a purpose","question":"Tell me about Estimates have a purpose","words":"Estimates have a purpose math","levels":{"6-8":"An estimate is a useful nearby answer, rather than a random guess.","9-11":"Rounding and known quantities can help produce an estimate before an exact calculation.","12-14":"Estimation trades precision for efficiency while retaining enough accuracy for a purpose; the acceptable error depends on the decision."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-averages-describe-different-things","title":"Averages describe different things","question":"Tell me about Averages describe different things","words":"Averages describe different things math","levels":{"6-8":"There is more than one way to describe a typical number.","9-11":"Mean, median and mode summarise a data set in different ways.","12-14":"The mean uses every value, the median uses ordering, and the mode uses frequency; outliers can affect these summaries differently."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-chance-is-not-a-promise","title":"Chance is not a promise","question":"Tell me about Chance is not a promise","words":"Chance is not a promise math","levels":{"6-8":"A likely event can still fail to happen.","9-11":"A fair coin has the same chance of heads on the next toss even after several tails.","12-14":"For independent trials with fixed probabilities, previous outcomes do not force a balancing result on the next trial."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-triangles-have-three-sides","title":"Triangles have three sides","question":"Tell me about Triangles have three sides","words":"Triangles have three sides math","levels":{"6-8":"A triangle has three straight sides.","9-11":"A flat triangle’s interior angles add to a straight angle, or 180 degrees.","12-14":"The 180-degree angle sum applies to Euclidean triangles; triangles drawn on curved surfaces can behave differently."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-squares-are-special-rectangles","title":"Squares are special rectangles","question":"Tell me about Squares are special rectangles","words":"Squares are special rectangles math","levels":{"6-8":"A square has four equal sides and four square corners.","9-11":"Every square is a rectangle because it meets the rectangle rule of four right angles.","12-14":"Classification can be inclusive: a square belongs to the rectangle family while also satisfying the extra condition of equal side lengths."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rectangles-come-in-many-proportions","title":"Rectangles come in many proportions","question":"Tell me about Rectangles come in many proportions","words":"Rectangles come in many proportions math","levels":{"6-8":"A rectangle does not have to look long and thin.","9-11":"Any quadrilateral with four right angles is a rectangle, including a square.","12-14":"Orientation and aspect ratio do not define rectangularity; the angle conditions are what determine membership of the class."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-circles-keep-a-constant-distance","title":"Circles keep a constant distance","question":"Tell me about Circles keep a constant distance","words":"Circles keep a constant distance math","levels":{"6-8":"Every point on a circle is the same distance from its centre.","9-11":"That centre-to-edge distance is the radius, and a diameter crosses the centre from edge to edge.","12-14":"A circle is defined by a constant distance from a fixed point; its diameter is twice its radius in Euclidean geometry."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-pi-connects-circles","title":"Pi connects circles","question":"Tell me about Pi connects circles","words":"Pi connects circles math","levels":{"6-8":"All circles share the same special circumference-to-diameter ratio.","9-11":"The number pi is the circumference of a circle divided by its diameter.","12-14":"Pi is an irrational constant, so its decimal representation continues without terminating or repeating, although measurements use approximations."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-perimeter-goes-around","title":"Perimeter goes around","question":"Tell me about Perimeter goes around","words":"Perimeter goes around math","levels":{"6-8":"Perimeter measures the distance around a shape.","9-11":"Adding all side lengths gives the perimeter of a polygon.","12-14":"Perimeter is a one-dimensional boundary measure, so shapes with equal perimeter can still enclose different areas."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-area-covers-a-surface","title":"Area covers a surface","question":"Tell me about Area covers a surface","words":"Area covers a surface math","levels":{"6-8":"Area tells how much flat space a shape covers.","9-11":"A rectangle’s area can be counted in equal squares or calculated as length times width.","12-14":"Area is measured in square units; multiplying two lengths changes the unit dimension, not just the numerical value."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-volume-fills-space","title":"Volume fills space","question":"Tell me about Volume fills space","words":"Volume fills space math","levels":{"6-8":"Volume tells how much three-dimensional space something occupies.","9-11":"A rectangular box’s volume is length times width times height.","12-14":"Volume uses cubic units, and equal volumes do not require equal surface areas or identical shapes."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-cube-has-six-faces","title":"A cube has six faces","question":"Tell me about A cube has six faces","words":"A cube has six faces math","levels":{"6-8":"A cube has six square faces.","9-11":"A cube also has twelve edges and eight vertices, where edges meet.","12-14":"A cube is a regular hexahedron with congruent square faces; its face, edge and vertex counts satisfy Euler’s polyhedron relation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-sphere-has-no-corners","title":"A sphere has no corners","question":"Tell me about A sphere has no corners","words":"A sphere has no corners math","levels":{"6-8":"A ball-shaped sphere has no edges or corners.","9-11":"Every point on the surface of a perfect sphere is the same distance from its centre.","12-14":"A sphere is a curved surface defined by a constant radius, distinct from the solid ball that includes its interior."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-nets-fold-into-solids","title":"Nets fold into solids","question":"Tell me about Nets fold into solids","words":"Nets fold into solids math","levels":{"6-8":"A flat pattern can fold into a three-dimensional shape.","9-11":"A net shows connected faces arranged so they can fold into a solid without gaps.","12-14":"Different valid nets can represent the same polyhedron; not every arrangement with the right number of faces folds successfully."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-symmetry-can-use-a-mirror","title":"Symmetry can use a mirror","question":"Tell me about Symmetry can use a mirror","words":"Symmetry can use a mirror math","levels":{"6-8":"Some shapes match themselves when reflected across a line.","9-11":"A line of symmetry divides a shape into mirror-image parts.","12-14":"Reflection symmetry preserves a figure under a particular transformation; visual balance alone does not guarantee mathematical symmetry."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rotation-can-preserve-a-shape","title":"Rotation can preserve a shape","question":"Tell me about Rotation can preserve a shape","words":"Rotation can preserve a shape math","levels":{"6-8":"Some shapes look unchanged after a turn smaller than a full turn.","9-11":"A square matches itself after a quarter turn, showing rotational symmetry.","12-14":"Rotational symmetry is described by turns that map a shape onto itself; its order counts matches during one full revolution."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-tessellations-leave-no-gaps","title":"Tessellations leave no gaps","question":"Tell me about Tessellations leave no gaps","words":"Tessellations leave no gaps math","levels":{"6-8":"Some shapes can cover a flat surface without gaps or overlaps.","9-11":"Squares, equilateral triangles and regular hexagons can each tile a plane by themselves.","12-14":"Regular tessellations depend on angles fitting around a point; this explains why regular pentagons cannot tile the Euclidean plane alone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-scaling-changes-size-in-a-pattern","title":"Scaling changes size in a pattern","question":"Tell me about Scaling changes size in a pattern","words":"Scaling changes size in a pattern math","levels":{"6-8":"Making a drawing twice as wide and twice as tall makes it bigger in both directions.","9-11":"Doubling every length multiplies a flat shape’s area by four, not two.","12-14":"Under uniform scale factor k, lengths scale by k, areas by k² and volumes by k³. The factor applies equally in every direction."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-congruent-shapes-fit-exactly","title":"Congruent shapes fit exactly","question":"Tell me about Congruent shapes fit exactly","words":"Congruent shapes fit exactly math","levels":{"6-8":"Congruent shapes have the same size and shape.","9-11":"You may need to turn, slide or flip one congruent shape to match another.","12-14":"Congruence permits rigid transformations while preserving all distances and angles; enlargement generally produces similarity instead."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-similar-shapes-keep-proportions","title":"Similar shapes keep proportions","question":"Tell me about Similar shapes keep proportions","words":"Similar shapes keep proportions math","levels":{"6-8":"Similar shapes have the same shape but may have different sizes.","9-11":"Corresponding angles match and corresponding lengths share one scale factor.","12-14":"Similarity preserves angle measures and length ratios, while absolute lengths, areas and volumes may change."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-coordinates-locate-a-point","title":"Coordinates locate a point","question":"Tell me about Coordinates locate a point","words":"Coordinates locate a point math","levels":{"6-8":"A pair of numbers can tell where a point is on a grid.","9-11":"In ordinary Cartesian coordinates, the first value gives horizontal position and the second vertical position.","12-14":"Coordinates locate points relative to chosen axes and units; the same physical location can have different coordinates in another system."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-parallel-lines-stay-apart","title":"Parallel lines stay apart","question":"Tell me about Parallel lines stay apart","words":"Parallel lines stay apart math","levels":{"6-8":"Parallel straight lines on a flat plane do not meet.","9-11":"Parallel lines keep the same separation when measured perpendicularly.","12-14":"In Euclidean geometry, distinct coplanar parallel lines have the same direction; statements about parallelism depend on the geometry used."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-right-angles-measure-a-quarter-turn","title":"Right angles measure a quarter turn","question":"Tell me about Right angles measure a quarter turn","words":"Right angles measure a quarter turn math","levels":{"6-8":"A square corner is a right angle.","9-11":"Four right angles fit into one full turn.","12-14":"A right angle is 90 degrees, independent of the lengths of its arms or the orientation of the drawing."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-an-algorithm-is-a-plan","title":"An algorithm is a plan","question":"Tell me about An algorithm is a plan","words":"An algorithm is a plan tech","levels":{"6-8":"An algorithm is a clear set of steps for a task.","9-11":"Recipes, sorting methods and computer procedures can all be described as algorithms.","12-14":"An algorithm specifies a procedure; analysing its correctness and resource use is different from merely writing it in a programming language."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-programs-express-instructions","title":"Programs express instructions","question":"Tell me about Programs express instructions","words":"Programs express instructions tech","levels":{"6-8":"A computer program gives instructions in a language a computer system can process.","9-11":"Programming languages let people describe data and operations using agreed rules.","12-14":"A program’s syntax determines whether it is well-formed, while its semantics describe what the instructions mean and do."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-bug-is-not-usually-an-insect","title":"A bug is not usually an insect","question":"Tell me about A bug is not usually an insect","words":"A bug is not usually an insect tech","levels":{"6-8":"A software bug is a mistake that makes a program behave unexpectedly.","9-11":"Bugs can come from incorrect logic, mistaken assumptions or other problems in a program.","12-14":"Software defects are not limited to typing errors; a syntactically valid program can still implement an incorrect idea."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-debugging-uses-evidence","title":"Debugging uses evidence","question":"Tell me about Debugging uses evidence","words":"Debugging uses evidence tech","levels":{"6-8":"Debugging means investigating why a program is not doing what was intended.","9-11":"A developer can compare expected and actual behaviour, then test a possible fix.","12-14":"Effective debugging isolates causes through observation and controlled changes rather than replacing unrelated code at random."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-loops-repeat-work","title":"Loops repeat work","question":"Tell me about Loops repeat work","words":"Loops repeat work tech","levels":{"6-8":"A loop tells a program to repeat some instructions.","9-11":"Loops can repeat a fixed number of times or continue while a condition is true.","12-14":"Iteration reduces duplication, but the termination condition matters: a loop can continue indefinitely if its exit condition is never reached."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-conditions-choose-a-path","title":"Conditions choose a path","question":"Tell me about Conditions choose a path","words":"Conditions choose a path tech","levels":{"6-8":"A program can choose what to do using a condition.","9-11":"An if-statement runs particular instructions when a test is true.","12-14":"Conditional control flow depends on evaluated predicates, allowing one program to respond differently to different inputs."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-variables-hold-values","title":"Variables hold values","question":"Tell me about Variables hold values","words":"Variables hold values tech","levels":{"6-8":"A variable is a name a program uses for a value.","9-11":"A variable’s value may change as instructions run, depending on the language and declaration.","12-14":"Variables connect names with values or storage; scope and type rules determine how they can be used."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-inputs-can-come-from-sensors","title":"Inputs can come from sensors","question":"Tell me about Inputs can come from sensors","words":"Inputs can come from sensors tech","levels":{"6-8":"A computer can receive information from a keyboard, sensor or another system.","9-11":"Input gives a program data to process, while output communicates a result or action.","12-14":"Input and output are roles within a system, so one device’s output can become another device’s input."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-sensor-measures-something","title":"A sensor measures something","question":"Tell me about A sensor measures something","words":"A sensor measures something tech","levels":{"6-8":"A sensor helps a machine notice light, movement, temperature or another property.","9-11":"Sensors convert physical conditions into signals that a system can process.","12-14":"Sensor readings depend on calibration, placement, range and noise; digital output does not make a measurement automatically accurate."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-binary-uses-two-symbols","title":"Binary uses two symbols","question":"Tell me about Binary uses two symbols","words":"Binary uses two symbols tech","levels":{"6-8":"Binary is a number system that uses only zero and one.","9-11":"Positions in a binary number represent powers of two, rather than powers of ten.","12-14":"Binary notation is distinct from the physical implementation of bits, which can use electrical, magnetic, optical or other states."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-bit-is-a-two-way-choice","title":"A bit is a two-way choice","question":"Tell me about A bit is a two-way choice","words":"A bit is a two-way choice tech","levels":{"6-8":"A bit can represent one of two possible values.","9-11":"Groups of bits can encode numbers, text, pictures and other information.","12-14":"A bit is a unit of information with two distinguishable states; interpretation depends on the encoding and surrounding system."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-digital-pictures-use-pixels","title":"Digital pictures use pixels","question":"Tell me about Digital pictures use pixels","words":"Digital pictures use pixels tech","levels":{"6-8":"A digital picture can be built from many tiny picture elements.","9-11":"Pixels store colour information, and an image’s arrangement of pixels creates its visible detail.","12-14":"Pixel count affects sampling resolution, but image quality also depends on optics, processing, colour depth and display conditions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-compression-reduces-data-size","title":"Compression reduces data size","question":"Tell me about Compression reduces data size","words":"Compression reduces data size tech","levels":{"6-8":"Compression can make a file take up less storage space.","9-11":"Lossless compression keeps recoverable information, while lossy compression discards some information to save more space.","12-14":"Compression exploits redundancy or acceptable approximation; the best method depends on the data and the intended use."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-internet-connects-networks","title":"The internet connects networks","question":"Tell me about The internet connects networks","words":"The internet connects networks tech","levels":{"6-8":"The internet links many computer networks together.","9-11":"Devices exchange data across interconnected networks using shared communication rules.","12-14":"The internet is infrastructure based on interoperating networks; it is not identical to a single website, company or service."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-web-is-one-internet-service","title":"The web is one internet service","question":"Tell me about The web is one internet service","words":"The web is one internet service tech","levels":{"6-8":"Websites use the internet, but the internet does more than websites.","9-11":"Email and other services can also use internet connections without being the World Wide Web.","12-14":"The web uses linked resources and protocols such as HTTP, while the internet supports multiple application-layer services."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-messages-travel-in-packets","title":"Messages travel in packets","question":"Tell me about Messages travel in packets","words":"Messages travel in packets tech","levels":{"6-8":"A message can be split into small data pieces for its journey.","9-11":"Packets carry information across networks and are used by communication systems to deliver data.","12-14":"Packet-switched networks share transmission resources; delivery order, reliability and reassembly depend on the protocols involved."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-browsers-display-web-content","title":"Browsers display web content","question":"Tell me about Browsers display web content","words":"Browsers display web content tech","levels":{"6-8":"A browser is the software you use to open websites.","9-11":"Browsers interpret web resources such as HTML, CSS and JavaScript to present pages.","12-14":"A browser combines networking, parsing, layout and execution systems; a search engine is a service accessed through it, not the browser itself."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-servers-answer-requests","title":"Servers answer requests","question":"Tell me about Servers answer requests","words":"Servers answer requests tech","levels":{"6-8":"A server can send information when another computer asks for it.","9-11":"Web servers respond to browser requests with resources such as pages, images or data.","12-14":"Client and server describe roles in an interaction rather than necessarily identifying particular sizes or types of computers."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ai-learns-patterns-from-examples","title":"AI learns patterns from examples","question":"Tell me about AI learns patterns from examples","words":"AI learns patterns from examples tech","levels":{"6-8":"Some AI systems learn patterns from examples instead of being told every answer.","9-11":"A machine-learning model adjusts using training data, then applies learned patterns to new inputs.","12-14":"Training produces a model whose outputs depend on data, objectives and architecture; fluent output is not proof of understanding or correctness."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-ai-answers-need-checking","title":"AI answers need checking","question":"Tell me about AI answers need checking","words":"AI answers need checking tech","levels":{"6-8":"An AI can sound confident and still make a mistake.","9-11":"AI systems can generate incorrect information, so important claims need suitable evidence and checking.","12-14":"Generative models can produce plausible unsupported statements; source verification and domain expertise remain necessary for assessing reliability."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-paint-and-light-mix-differently","title":"Paint and light mix differently","question":"Tell me about Paint and light mix differently","words":"Paint and light mix differently art","levels":{"6-8":"Mixing coloured paints does not work the same way as mixing coloured lights.","9-11":"Paint pigments absorb parts of the light, while coloured lights add light together.","12-14":"Subtractive pigment mixing and additive light mixing use different physical mechanisms, so the same colour names do not predict identical results."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-colour-depends-on-lighting","title":"Colour depends on lighting","question":"Tell me about Colour depends on lighting","words":"Colour depends on lighting art","levels":{"6-8":"A coloured object can look different under different lights.","9-11":"The light reaching your eyes depends on both the illumination and how the object reflects it.","12-14":"Perceived colour combines spectral illumination, surface reflectance and visual processing; it is not solely a fixed label attached to an object."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-perspective-can-suggest-depth","title":"Perspective can suggest depth","question":"Tell me about Perspective can suggest depth","words":"Perspective can suggest depth art","levels":{"6-8":"Artists can make a flat picture look as if it has depth.","9-11":"In linear perspective, parallel lines going away from the viewer can appear to meet at a vanishing point.","12-14":"Perspective is a representational system based on projection; converging image lines do not mean the physical parallel lines actually intersect."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-overlapping-gives-a-clue","title":"Overlapping gives a clue","question":"Tell me about Overlapping gives a clue","words":"Overlapping gives a clue art","levels":{"6-8":"One shape partly covering another can make it seem closer.","9-11":"Artists use overlap as one clue that helps viewers interpret depth in a flat image.","12-14":"Occlusion provides relative depth information, but it does not by itself reveal exact distances or the full three-dimensional structure."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-warm-and-cool-colours-are-a-convention","title":"Warm and cool colours are a convention","question":"Tell me about Warm and cool colours are a convention","words":"Warm and cool colours are a convention art","levels":{"6-8":"Artists often call reds warm and blues cool.","9-11":"Warm and cool are useful colour descriptions in art, not measurements of an object’s temperature.","12-14":"Artistic colour-temperature categories describe visual associations and relationships; they differ from the physical temperature of stars or objects."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-texture-can-be-real-or-pictured","title":"Texture can be real or pictured","question":"Tell me about Texture can be real or pictured","words":"Texture can be real or pictured art","levels":{"6-8":"A picture can look rough even when the paper feels smooth.","9-11":"Art can use actual surface texture or create the visual impression of texture with marks and shading.","12-14":"Tactile texture and implied texture are different properties, allowing visual representation to suggest materials without reproducing their physical surface."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-prints-can-reverse-an-image","title":"Prints can reverse an image","question":"Tell me about Prints can reverse an image","words":"Prints can reverse an image art","levels":{"6-8":"A print can show a mirror version of its printing surface.","9-11":"In many printmaking methods, transferring the image flips left and right.","12-14":"Direct contact printing often reverses orientation, so artists plan lettering and composition with the transfer process in mind."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-negative-space-is-still-part","title":"Negative space is still part","question":"Tell me about Negative space is still part","words":"Negative space is still part art","levels":{"6-8":"The empty-looking space around a shape helps define it.","9-11":"Artists can study negative space, the areas around and between subjects, as well as the subjects themselves.","12-14":"Figure–ground relationships make negative space an active compositional element rather than simply unused background."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-patterns-can-repeat-a-motif","title":"Patterns can repeat a motif","question":"Tell me about Patterns can repeat a motif","words":"Patterns can repeat a motif art","levels":{"6-8":"A small shape or idea can repeat to form a pattern.","9-11":"The repeated element in a design can be called a motif, with spacing and variation affecting the result.","12-14":"Repetition can establish visual rhythm, while controlled variation changes emphasis without necessarily removing the underlying organising rule."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-sculpture-occupies-space","title":"Sculpture occupies space","question":"Tell me about Sculpture occupies space","words":"Sculpture occupies space art","levels":{"6-8":"A sculpture has form that can be viewed from different directions.","9-11":"Unlike a flat drawing, a sculpture often uses three-dimensional volume and surrounding space.","12-14":"Sculptural experience can depend on viewpoint, material, scale and installation; a single photograph may show only one aspect of the work."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-clay-can-become-a-vessel","title":"Clay can become a vessel","question":"Tell me about Clay can become a vessel","words":"Clay can become a vessel art","levels":{"6-8":"People can shape clay into bowls, pots and other forms.","9-11":"Ceramic traditions combine material knowledge with design, practical needs and cultural meaning.","12-14":"A vessel can provide evidence about technology, use and exchange, but its form alone does not identify every detail of its maker’s life."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-weaving-interlaces-threads","title":"Weaving interlaces threads","question":"Tell me about Weaving interlaces threads","words":"Weaving interlaces threads art","levels":{"6-8":"Woven fabric is made by crossing sets of threads.","9-11":"Warp threads run along the cloth, while weft threads pass across them.","12-14":"Interlacement patterns affect a woven textile’s structure; weaving differs from knitting, which forms fabric through connected loops."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-beat-and-a-rhythm-differ","title":"A beat and a rhythm differ","question":"Tell me about A beat and a rhythm differ","words":"A beat and a rhythm differ art","levels":{"6-8":"A steady beat is not the same as the pattern you clap over it.","9-11":"Beat provides a regular pulse, while rhythm arranges sounds and silences in time.","12-14":"Rhythm describes temporal organisation; a musical passage can have complex rhythms over a steady underlying beat."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-instruments-have-different-timbres","title":"Instruments have different timbres","question":"Tell me about Instruments have different timbres","words":"Instruments have different timbres art","levels":{"6-8":"Two instruments can play the same note and still sound different.","9-11":"The quality that helps distinguish a flute from a violin is called timbre.","12-14":"Timbre depends on spectral content and how sound develops over time, not simply on fundamental frequency or loudness."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-silence-can-shape-music","title":"Silence can shape music","question":"Tell me about Silence can shape music","words":"Silence can shape music art","levels":{"6-8":"A pause can be part of a musical pattern.","9-11":"Rests tell performers when not to make a sound, helping shape rhythm and phrasing.","12-14":"Silence functions structurally in music through duration, expectation and contrast, rather than being merely a failure to perform."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-stories-have-viewpoints","title":"Stories have viewpoints","question":"Tell me about Stories have viewpoints","words":"Stories have viewpoints art","levels":{"6-8":"Different characters can describe the same event differently.","9-11":"A story’s point of view affects which details readers receive and how events are presented.","12-14":"Narrative perspective controls access to information and interpretation; a narrator’s account need not be complete or fully reliable."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fiction-can-use-real-ideas","title":"Fiction can use real ideas","question":"Tell me about Fiction can use real ideas","words":"Fiction can use real ideas art","levels":{"6-8":"An invented story can explore real feelings and scientific ideas.","9-11":"Fictional characters and events can help readers think through questions without making the story a factual report.","12-14":"Fiction can support conceptual exploration, but invented plot events are not evidence that the same events occurred in the real world."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-an-illustration-is-an-interpretation","title":"An illustration is an interpretation","question":"Tell me about An illustration is an interpretation","words":"An illustration is an interpretation art","levels":{"6-8":"An illustration shows what an artist chose to include.","9-11":"Illustrations can clarify an idea, but choices about scale, detail and viewpoint affect what they communicate.","12-14":"A visual explanation is a designed representation; readers should distinguish schematic emphasis from literal physical appearance."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-photographs-can-be-cropped","title":"Photographs can be cropped","question":"Tell me about Photographs can be cropped","words":"Photographs can be cropped art","levels":{"6-8":"A photograph may show only part of a bigger scene.","9-11":"Cropping changes what viewers can see, which can change how they interpret an image.","12-14":"Photographs record selected viewpoints and moments; framing, cropping and context influence the conclusions an image can support."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-museums-preserve-context","title":"Museums preserve context","question":"Tell me about Museums preserve context","words":"Museums preserve context art","levels":{"6-8":"A museum object has a story beyond how it looks.","9-11":"Records about where an object came from and how it was used help people understand it.","12-14":"Provenance and contextual evidence are important to interpretation, ownership history and responsible stewardship of cultural collections."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kenya-crosses-the-equator","title":"Kenya crosses the Equator","question":"Tell me about Kenya crosses the Equator","words":"Kenya crosses the Equator kenya","levels":{"6-8":"The Equator passes through Kenya.","9-11":"Kenya includes land both north and south of the Equator, the imaginary line at zero degrees latitude.","12-14":"The Equator is a geographical reference rather than a physical line; Kenya’s position spans both hemispheres."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kenya-meets-the-indian-ocean","title":"Kenya meets the Indian Ocean","question":"Tell me about Kenya meets the Indian Ocean","words":"Kenya meets the Indian Ocean kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Kenya has a coast beside the Indian Ocean.","9-11":"The coast connects Kenyan landscapes and communities with the wider Indian Ocean region.","12-14":"Kenya’s coastline links marine ecosystems and long histories of travel and exchange with inland environments and communities."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kiswahili-is-a-national-language","title":"Kiswahili is a national language","question":"Tell me about Kiswahili is a national language","words":"Kiswahili is a national language kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Kiswahili is Kenya’s national language.","9-11":"Kenya’s Constitution names Kiswahili as the national language and Kiswahili and English as official languages.","12-14":"National-language and official-language status are distinct constitutional descriptions; Kenyan language diversity extends beyond these two official languages."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kenya-protects-language-diversity","title":"Kenya protects language diversity","question":"Tell me about Kenya protects language diversity","words":"Kenya protects language diversity kenya","levels":{"6-8":"People in Kenya use many different languages.","9-11":"The Constitution recognises the importance of indigenous languages, Kenyan Sign Language and accessible communication.","12-14":"Constitutional language provisions include protecting diversity and promoting accessible formats; no single language describes every Kenyan community."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-counties-are-part-of-devolution","title":"Counties are part of devolution","question":"Tell me about Counties are part of devolution","words":"Counties are part of devolution kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Kenya is divided into 47 counties.","9-11":"County governments are part of Kenya’s devolved system alongside the national government.","12-14":"Devolution distributes specified public functions between national and county levels; county boundaries are administrative, not complete cultural descriptions."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mount-kenya-was-a-volcano","title":"Mount Kenya was a volcano","question":"Tell me about Mount Kenya was a volcano","words":"Mount Kenya was a volcano kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Mount Kenya is an ancient volcano that is no longer active.","9-11":"The mountain’s present peaks are remnants of an extinct volcano shaped by erosion.","12-14":"Mount Kenya’s volcanic origin and later erosion explain its rugged form; the visible peaks are not an unchanged original volcanic cone."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mount-kenya-has-habitat-zones","title":"Mount Kenya has habitat zones","question":"Tell me about Mount Kenya has habitat zones","words":"Mount Kenya has habitat zones kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Plants and habitats change as you travel higher on Mount Kenya.","9-11":"Forested slopes give way to different high-altitude vegetation as conditions change with elevation.","12-14":"Altitudinal variation in temperature, moisture and exposure supports distinct ecological zones across Mount Kenya’s landscape."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-mount-kenya-connects-wildlife-places","title":"Mount Kenya connects wildlife places","question":"Tell me about Mount Kenya connects wildlife places","words":"Mount Kenya connects wildlife places kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Protected landscapes around Mount Kenya connect different habitats.","9-11":"Wildlife corridors link the mountain area with lower-lying conservation landscapes, helping elephants move between them.","12-14":"Connectivity between protected areas matters because animals use resources across landscapes rather than remaining inside human-drawn boundaries."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-lamu-is-a-living-old-town","title":"Lamu is a living old town","question":"Tell me about Lamu is a living old town","words":"Lamu is a living old town kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Lamu Old Town is a historic place where people still live.","9-11":"Lamu preserves Swahili urban traditions as a living settlement, rather than only as empty ruins.","12-14":"The town’s significance includes continuing social and cultural life alongside historic buildings and town planning."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-lamu-doors-show-craftsmanship","title":"Lamu doors show craftsmanship","question":"Tell me about Lamu doors show craftsmanship","words":"Lamu doors show craftsmanship kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Some Lamu buildings have beautifully carved wooden doors.","9-11":"Traditional Lamu architecture includes carved doors, courtyards and materials such as coral stone and mangrove timber.","12-14":"Architectural details in Lamu reflect local techniques and cultural exchanges; a single decorative feature should not stand for all Swahili heritage."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fort-jesus-guards-a-port-history","title":"Fort Jesus guards a port history","question":"Tell me about Fort Jesus guards a port history","words":"Fort Jesus guards a port history kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Fort Jesus is a historic fort in Mombasa.","9-11":"Portuguese builders constructed Fort Jesus in the late sixteenth century to protect the port of Mombasa.","12-14":"Fort Jesus records changing control and cultural encounters around Indian Ocean trade, rather than representing only one community’s history."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-kaya-forests-carry-living-heritage","title":"Kaya forests carry living heritage","question":"Tell me about Kaya forests carry living heritage","words":"Kaya forests carry living heritage kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Kaya forests are sacred places for Mijikenda communities.","9-11":"The Sacred Mijikenda Kaya Forests combine forest environments with important ancestral places and traditions.","12-14":"Cultural practices and stewardship have helped preserve Kaya landscapes; respectful interpretation includes their living significance, not just their trees."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-gedi-was-a-swahili-town","title":"Gedi was a Swahili town","question":"Tell me about Gedi was a Swahili town","words":"Gedi was a Swahili town kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Gedi’s ruins preserve parts of an old Swahili town.","9-11":"Buildings and streets at Gedi provide evidence of a substantial coastal settlement and its connections across the Indian Ocean.","12-14":"Gedi’s archaeology documents urban life, trade and cultural exchange; abandoned buildings do not mean their inhabitants left no recoverable history."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-gedi-managed-water","title":"Gedi managed water","question":"Tell me about Gedi managed water","words":"Gedi managed water kenya","levels":{"6-8":"People at Gedi built systems for managing water.","9-11":"Wells and other water infrastructure show that the historic town needed organised ways to supply and handle water.","12-14":"Water-management remains offer evidence of planning and technology, helping archaeologists investigate how urban life was sustained."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-thimlich-ohinga-used-dry-stone","title":"Thimlich Ohinga used dry stone","question":"Tell me about Thimlich Ohinga used dry stone","words":"Thimlich Ohinga used dry stone kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Thimlich Ohinga has walls built from carefully arranged stones.","9-11":"Its builders made strong enclosures without using mortar to glue the stones together.","12-14":"Interlocking dry-stone construction at Thimlich Ohinga demonstrates structural knowledge and communal settlement traditions in the Lake Victoria region."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-rift-valley-lakes-support-bird-life","title":"Rift Valley lakes support bird life","question":"Tell me about Rift Valley lakes support bird life","words":"Rift Valley lakes support bird life kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Some Kenyan Rift Valley lakes provide important places for birds.","9-11":"Lake Bogoria, Lake Nakuru and Lake Elementaita form the Kenya Lake System World Heritage property.","12-14":"The three alkaline lakes and their surroundings support connected ecological processes, including important feeding and breeding habitats for birds."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-turkana-preserves-ancient-clues","title":"Turkana preserves ancient clues","question":"Tell me about Turkana preserves ancient clues","words":"Turkana preserves ancient clues kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Fossils around Lake Turkana help scientists study the distant past.","9-11":"Deposits around the lake contain evidence of earlier environments, animals and human ancestors.","12-14":"Fossils and geological layers in the Turkana Basin help reconstruct environmental and evolutionary history, with conclusions based on contextual evidence."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-turkana-has-island-parks","title":"Turkana has island parks","question":"Tell me about Turkana has island parks","words":"Turkana has island parks kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Protected areas at Lake Turkana include islands as well as mainland places.","9-11":"The Lake Turkana National Parks property includes Sibiloi and Central and South Island national parks.","12-14":"The protected property combines contrasting terrestrial and aquatic settings, illustrating why conservation can encompass connected habitat types."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-wangari-maathai-earned-a-nobel-prize","title":"Wangari Maathai earned a Nobel Prize","question":"Tell me about Wangari Maathai earned a Nobel Prize","words":"Wangari Maathai earned a Nobel Prize kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Kenyan scientist Wangari Maathai received the Nobel Peace Prize.","9-11":"In 2004, Wangari Maathai became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize.","12-14":"The award recognised links among sustainable development, democracy and peace, showing that environmental action can have wider social significance."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-the-green-belt-movement-grew-locally","title":"The Green Belt Movement grew locally","question":"Tell me about The Green Belt Movement grew locally","words":"The Green Belt Movement grew locally kenya","levels":{"6-8":"Wangari Maathai helped communities organise tree planting.","9-11":"She founded the Green Belt Movement in 1977, connecting tree planting with community action.","12-14":"The movement linked ecological restoration with women’s participation and livelihoods, rather than treating trees as separate from people’s lives."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-observations-and-explanations-differ","title":"Observations and explanations differ","question":"Tell me about Observations and explanations differ","words":"Observations and explanations differ science","levels":{"6-8":"Seeing a wet path is an observation; guessing why it is wet is an explanation.","9-11":"An observation records what was noticed, while an explanation proposes a reason for it.","12-14":"Separating evidence from interpretation helps reveal alternative explanations and prevents a plausible story from being mistaken for a direct measurement."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-one-clue-can-fit-several-stories","title":"One clue can fit several stories","question":"Tell me about One clue can fit several stories","words":"One clue can fit several stories science","levels":{"6-8":"A single clue can have more than one explanation.","9-11":"A wet garden might follow rain, watering or another source, so one observation may not identify the cause.","12-14":"Underdetermination means evidence can be compatible with multiple hypotheses; additional observations can help distinguish among them."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-prediction-can-be-tested","title":"A prediction can be tested","question":"Tell me about A prediction can be tested","words":"A prediction can be tested science","levels":{"6-8":"A prediction says what you expect to notice under particular conditions.","9-11":"Scientists can compare a prediction with observations to see whether an idea is supported.","12-14":"Testable predictions connect explanations with possible evidence, including results that could challenge the explanation."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-fair-comparisons-control-differences","title":"Fair comparisons control differences","question":"Tell me about Fair comparisons control differences","words":"Fair comparisons control differences science","levels":{"6-8":"A fair comparison changes only what is needed for the question.","9-11":"Keeping other important conditions similar makes it easier to interpret the effect of a chosen change.","12-14":"Controlling confounding variables strengthens causal inference, though real investigations may require more complex designs than changing only one thing."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-repeated-measurements-can-differ","title":"Repeated measurements can differ","question":"Tell me about Repeated measurements can differ","words":"Repeated measurements can differ science","levels":{"6-8":"Measuring the same thing twice may give slightly different results.","9-11":"Variation can come from instruments, methods, changing conditions or the observer.","12-14":"Repeated measurements reveal aspects of precision and variability; agreement does not by itself prove freedom from systematic error."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-precision-is-not-accuracy","title":"Precision is not accuracy","question":"Tell me about Precision is not accuracy","words":"Precision is not accuracy science","levels":{"6-8":"A very exact-looking number can still be wrong.","9-11":"Precision describes how closely repeated measurements agree, while accuracy concerns closeness to the true or accepted value.","12-14":"A biased instrument can produce highly repeatable but inaccurate readings, so decimal places alone do not establish measurement quality."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-units-give-numbers-meaning","title":"Units give numbers meaning","question":"Tell me about Units give numbers meaning","words":"Units give numbers meaning science","levels":{"6-8":"A measurement needs a unit as well as a number.","9-11":"Ten centimetres and ten metres are different lengths even though the number is the same.","12-14":"Units specify the scale and physical dimension of a measurement; calculations must preserve consistent relationships among them."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-model-leaves-things-out","title":"A model leaves things out","question":"Tell me about A model leaves things out","words":"A model leaves things out science","levels":{"6-8":"A model shows useful parts of something without copying everything.","9-11":"A globe, diagram or simulation simplifies reality to help answer particular questions.","12-14":"Models are purpose-dependent representations whose assumptions and limits determine which conclusions they can support."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-scale-model-keeps-ratios","title":"A scale model keeps ratios","question":"Tell me about A scale model keeps ratios","words":"A scale model keeps ratios science","levels":{"6-8":"A scale model changes size while keeping selected proportions.","9-11":"A model can preserve distance ratios even when colours, materials or other features are simplified.","12-14":"A model’s scale applies to specified dimensions; an illustration may use different scales for object sizes and distances."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-correlation-is-not-a-cause","title":"Correlation is not a cause","question":"Tell me about Correlation is not a cause","words":"Correlation is not a cause science","levels":{"6-8":"Two things changing together does not prove that one caused the other.","9-11":"A shared cause or coincidence can make two patterns appear connected.","12-14":"Correlation alone cannot distinguish direct causation, reverse causation, confounding and chance without additional evidence or a suitable study design."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-samples-represent-a-larger-group","title":"Samples represent a larger group","question":"Tell me about Samples represent a larger group","words":"Samples represent a larger group science","levels":{"6-8":"A sample is a smaller set studied to learn about something larger.","9-11":"A sample can mislead if it leaves out important parts of the population being studied.","12-14":"Sampling method affects representativeness; a large biased sample does not automatically become reliable simply because it contains many observations."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-graph-chooses-a-scale","title":"A graph chooses a scale","question":"Tell me about A graph chooses a scale","words":"A graph chooses a scale science","levels":{"6-8":"The scale on a graph affects how its pattern looks.","9-11":"Changing an axis range can make the same difference appear visually large or small.","12-14":"Graph interpretation requires checking axes, units, intervals and baselines rather than judging only the apparent steepness or height of marks."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-missing-data-is-not-zero","title":"Missing data is not zero","question":"Tell me about Missing data is not zero","words":"Missing data is not zero science","levels":{"6-8":"No recorded value does not necessarily mean the value was zero.","9-11":"A blank entry may mean a measurement was missed, unavailable or not applicable.","12-14":"Treating missing observations as numerical zero can introduce error; the reason for missingness affects valid analysis."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-an-outlier-needs-investigation","title":"An outlier needs investigation","question":"Tell me about An outlier needs investigation","words":"An outlier needs investigation science","levels":{"6-8":"A surprising result might be a mistake, or it might be a real clue.","9-11":"An unusual measurement should be checked rather than automatically erased.","12-14":"Outliers can reflect error, rare events or an incomplete model; transparent investigation is preferable to removal solely because a result is inconvenient."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-scientists-revise-explanations","title":"Scientists revise explanations","question":"Tell me about Scientists revise explanations","words":"Scientists revise explanations science","levels":{"6-8":"A good explanation can change when stronger evidence appears.","9-11":"Scientific knowledge develops as observations, methods and competing explanations improve.","12-14":"Revision is part of evidence-based inquiry, not proof that all explanations are equally unsupported or permanently unknowable."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-agreement-needs-independent-evidence","title":"Agreement needs independent evidence","question":"Tell me about Agreement needs independent evidence","words":"Agreement needs independent evidence science","levels":{"6-8":"Two reports are not independent if one simply copied the other.","9-11":"Several sources repeating one original claim do not create several separate observations.","12-14":"Evidence independence matters: duplicated claims can create an illusion of confirmation without adding new data or verification."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-classification-uses-chosen-features","title":"Classification uses chosen features","question":"Tell me about Classification uses chosen features","words":"Classification uses chosen features science","levels":{"6-8":"Things can be grouped in different ways for different questions.","9-11":"The same objects might be sorted by colour, material or use, producing different useful groups.","12-14":"Classification criteria determine categories; a useful grouping should be explicit and appropriate to the purpose rather than assumed to be universal."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-a-diagram-may-not-show-real-size","title":"A diagram may not show real size","question":"Tell me about A diagram may not show real size","words":"A diagram may not show real size science","levels":{"6-8":"A drawing can make tiny parts large enough to see.","9-11":"Scientific illustrations sometimes change scale or colour to highlight important features.","12-14":"Schematic representations prioritise explanatory relationships, so dimensions and colours should not be treated as literal unless the diagram states that they are."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-evidence-has-a-history","title":"Evidence has a history","question":"Tell me about Evidence has a history","words":"Evidence has a history science","levels":{"6-8":"A useful observation includes information about how it was collected.","9-11":"Recording the method, date, place and conditions helps other people interpret a result.","12-14":"Data provenance and documented procedures make evidence more traceable, support reproducibility and expose limits that a detached number would hide."},"sections":[]},{"id":"fact-uncertainty-can-be-described","title":"Uncertainty can be described","question":"Tell me about Uncertainty can be described","words":"Uncertainty can be described science","levels":{"6-8":"Saying what is not yet known can make an explanation clearer.","9-11":"Scientists describe uncertainty to show how strongly evidence supports a conclusion.","12-14":"Uncertainty is not the same as total ignorance: it can be quantified or characterised while still allowing well-supported comparisons and decisions."},"sections":[]}];
